(누구) 입에 붙이다 (누구) 입에 붙이다 [(nugu) ibe butida] /stick something to (someone's) lips — To share food with others./
-ㄴ -ㄴ [-ㄴ] /-n — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when ordering in a friendly manner./-n — An ending of a word that makes the preceding statement function as an adnominal phrase and indicates an event or action having occurred in the past./-n — An ending of a word that makes the preceding statement function as an adnominal phrase and indicates that an event or action has been completed and its state continues./-n — An ending of a word that makes the preceding statement function as an adnominal phrase and refers to the present state./
-ㄴ 것 -ㄴ 것 [-ㄴ geos] /-n geot — An expression used to enable a non-noun word to be used as a noun in a sentence or to be used in front of '이다' (be)./
-ㄴ 것 같다 -ㄴ 것 같다 [-ㄴ geos gatda] /-n geot gatda — An expression used to indicate that the statement is a guess./
-ㄴ 결과 -ㄴ 결과 [-ㄴ gyeolgwa] /-n gyeolgwa — An expression used to indicate that a situation occurred as a result of doing a certain act mentioned in the preceding statement./
-ㄴ 김에 -ㄴ 김에 [-ㄴ gime] /-n gime — An expression used to indicate that one is doing a certain act, after the act mentioned in the preceding statement or while doing a similar behavior at the same time./
-ㄴ 나머지 -ㄴ 나머지 [-ㄴ nameoji] /-n nameoji — An expression used to indicate that something is the result of a certain act or state mentioned in the preceding statement./
-ㄴ 다음에 -ㄴ 다음에 [-ㄴ daeume] /-n da-eume — An expression used to indicate that the time being described takes places after the completion of a certain work or process mentioned in the preceding statement./
-ㄴ 다음에야 -ㄴ 다음에야 [-ㄴ daeumeya] /-n da-eumeya — An expression used to indicate that a certain work or act must be completed, before the next situation becomes possible./-n da-eumeya — An expression used to indicate that given the state or under the situation expressed in the preceding sentence, something in the following sentence can never happen./
-ㄴ 대로 -ㄴ 대로 [-ㄴ daero] /-n daero — An expression used to indicate that something is the same as a previous act or state mentioned in the preceding statement./-n daero — An expression used to indicate that something is the same as the present state./
-ㄴ 대신에 -ㄴ 대신에 [-ㄴ daesine] /-n daesine — An expression used to indicate that the states and behaviors mentioned in the preceding statement and the following statement are different or opposites./-n daesine — An expression used to offer something similar to make up for a behavior or state mentioned in the preceding statement./
-ㄴ 데다가 -ㄴ 데다가 [-ㄴ dedaga] /-n dedaga — An expression used to indicate that a certain act or state has been added to its preceding act or state, making it worse or better./-n dedaga — An expression used to indicate that another fact or situation exists in addition to the apparent ones./
-ㄴ 동시에 -ㄴ 동시에 [-ㄴ dongsie] /-n dongsie — An expression used to indicate that a certain act or state mentioned in the preceding statement and following statement  happens at the same time./
-ㄴ 뒤에 -ㄴ 뒤에 [-ㄴ dwie] /-n dwie — An expression used to indicate that one does a certain act mentioned in the preceding statement and a while later, does another thing./
-ㄴ 듯 -ㄴ 듯 [-ㄴ deus] /-n deut — An expression used to guess that something said might be similar to the preceding state or situation./-n deut — An expression used to indicate that something is in a state which could be one of two possibilities./
-ㄴ 듯싶다 -ㄴ 듯싶다 [-ㄴ deussipda] /-n deutsipda — An expression used to guess the content of the preceding statement./
-ㄴ 듯하다 -ㄴ 듯하다 [-ㄴ deushada] /-n deutada — An expression used to guess the content of the preceding statement./
-ㄴ 마당에 -ㄴ 마당에 [-ㄴ madange] /-n madang-e — An expression used to indicate that one is in a situation or position where something mentioned in the preceding statement has already occurred./
-ㄴ 만큼 -ㄴ 만큼 [-ㄴ mankeum] /-n mankeum — An expression used to indicate that the following content is proportional to the preceding content or of a similar level or quantity./-n mankeum — An expression used to indicate that the preceding statement is the reason or basis for the following statement./
-ㄴ 모양이다 -ㄴ 모양이다 [-ㄴ moyangida] /-n moyang-ida — An expression used to guess that something is in progress or in a certain state, considering another fact or situation./
-ㄴ 반면에 -ㄴ 반면에 [-ㄴ banmyeone] /-n banmyeone — An expression used to indicate that the facts in the preceding statement and the following statement are opposites./
-ㄴ 법이다 -ㄴ 법이다 [-ㄴ beobida] /-n beobida — An expression used to indicate that the act or state mentioned in the preceding statement has been decided already or is reasonable./
-ㄴ 이상 -ㄴ 이상 [-ㄴ isang] /-n isang — An expression used to indicate that one must do something or accept a certain situation, because the preceding statement has been already decided./
-ㄴ 적이 없다 -ㄴ 적이 없다 [-ㄴ jeogi eobsda] /-n jeogi eopda — An expression used to indicate that the act or state mentioned in the preceding statement never existed./
-ㄴ 적이 있다 -ㄴ 적이 있다 [-ㄴ jeogi issda] /-n jeogi itda — An expression used to indicate that the act or state mentioned in the preceding statement existed at some point in time./
-ㄴ 줄 -ㄴ 줄 [-ㄴ jul] /-n jul — An expression used to indicate that one either knows or does not know a certain fact or state./
-ㄴ 지 -ㄴ 지 [-ㄴ ji] /-n ji — An expression used to indicate that a certain amount of time has passed since a certain act mentioned in the preceding statement occurred./
-ㄴ 채로 -ㄴ 채로 [-ㄴ chaero] /-n chaero — An expression used to indicate that the state remains the same as when a certain act mentioned in the preceding statement occurred./
-ㄴ 척하다 -ㄴ 척하다 [-ㄴ cheoghada] /-n cheokada — An expression used to indicate that one is pretending to do something or to be something, which is not true./
-ㄴ 체하다 -ㄴ 체하다 [-ㄴ chehada] /-n chehada — An expression used to indicate that one is pretending to do something or to be something, which is not true./
-ㄴ 탓 -ㄴ 탓 [-ㄴ tas] /-n tat — An expression used to indicate that the preceding statement is the reason or cause for the following negative phenomenon./
-ㄴ 편이다 -ㄴ 편이다 [-ㄴ pyeonida] /-n pyeonida — An expression used to indicate that something has a certain tendency or is classified as such, instead of being sure of it./
-ㄴ 후에 -ㄴ 후에 [-ㄴ hue] /-n hue — An expression used to indicate that one does a certain act mentioned in the preceding statement and a while later, does another thing./
-ㄴ- -ㄴ- [-ㄴ-] /-n- — An ending of a word referring to an event or action occurring in the present./
-ㄴ가 -ㄴ가 [-ㄴga] /-n-ga — (formal, moderately addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending referring to a question about a fact of the present./-n-ga — A sentence-final ending used by the speaker when questioning himself/herself or guessing./
-ㄴ가 보다 -ㄴ가 보다 [-ㄴga boda] /-n-ga boda — An expression used to guess about a fact mentioned in the preceding statement./
-ㄴ감 -ㄴ감 [-ㄴgam] /-n-gam — (formal, moderately addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to gently refute a certain fact./
-ㄴ걸 -ㄴ걸 [-ㄴgeol] /-n-geol — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when the speaker says a newly learned fact with admiration./-n-geol — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to explain one's thought or argument, or give a reason for it./
-ㄴ걸요 -ㄴ걸요 [-ㄴgeolyo] /-n-geolyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used when the speaker says a newly learned fact with admiration./-n-geolyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to explain one's thought or argument, or give a reason for it./
-ㄴ고 -ㄴ고 [-ㄴgo] /-n-go — (formal, highly addressee-lowering)(old-fashioned) A sentence-final ending used to politely ask about or criticize a fact of the present./-n-go — (formal, highly addressee-lowering)(old-fashioned) A sentence-final ending used to ask oneself something or guess about something./
-ㄴ다 -ㄴ다 [-ㄴda] /-nda — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to describe an event or fact of the present./-nda — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when asking oneself something./
-ㄴ다거나 -ㄴ다거나 [-ㄴdageona] /-ndageona — An expression used to explain something by listing some behaviors as examples./-ndageona — An expression used to choose one of two or more confronting behaviors./
-ㄴ다거든 -ㄴ다거든 [-ㄴdageodeun] /-ndageodeun — An expression used to say that another person's remarks are the condition or basis for the following statement./-ndageodeun — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) An expression used to conclude a sentence by quoting another person's remarks as a reason or basis./
-ㄴ다거든요 -ㄴ다거든요 [-ㄴdageodeunyo] /-ndageodeunnyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to say that the speaker's own behavior is based on another person's remark./
-ㄴ다고 -ㄴ다고 [-ㄴdago] /-ndago — A connective ending used when implying the purpose or intention of a certain action, or the reason or cause of a certain situation./-ndago — A connective ending used to indicate that the preceding statement is a quotation from a proverb./-ndago — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to emphatically state the speaker's thoughts or argument to the listener./-ndago — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to ask again about a fact that one heard and confirm it./-ndago — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to say something in such a way that one confirms and realizes that a certain fact is different from one's prediction./-ndago — An expression used to pass along what the speaker heard from another person, or to present the subject's thoughts, opinions, etc./
-ㄴ다고요 -ㄴ다고요 [-ㄴdagoyo] /-ndagoyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to repeat or stress the speaker's remark./-ndagoyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to confirm or question another person's remark./-ndagoyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to show off something to others./
-ㄴ다기에 -ㄴ다기에 [-ㄴdagie] /-ndagie — An expression used to say that something the speaker heard is the reason or basis for the following statement./
-ㄴ다길래 -ㄴ다길래 [-ㄴdagilrae] /-ndagillae — An expression used to say something the speaker heard as the reason or basis for the following statement./
-ㄴ다나 -ㄴ다나 [-ㄴdana] /-ndana — A sentence-final ending used to convey someone's remark, and imply that it is displeasing or annoying./-ndana — A sentence-final ending used to say something without certainty and in an indifferent attitude./
-ㄴ다남 -ㄴ다남 [-ㄴdanam] /-ndanam — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) An expression used to refute something lightly./
-ㄴ다네 -ㄴ다네 [-ㄴdane] /-ndane — (formal, moderately addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when the speaker says something that he/she already knows in an objective manner and informs the listener of it./-ndane — (formal, moderately addressee-lowering) An expression used to tell the listener something the speaker knows from having heard it./
-ㄴ다느냐 -ㄴ다느냐 [-ㄴdaneunya] /-ndaneunya — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) An expression used to ask the listener something the speaker heard from another person to cross-check the information./
-ㄴ다느니 -ㄴ다느니 [-ㄴdaneuni] /-ndaneuni — A connective ending referring to doing this and doing that./
-ㄴ다는 -ㄴ다는 [-ㄴdaneun] /-ndaneun — An expression used to quote someone, while modifying the following noun./
-ㄴ다는 것이 -ㄴ다는 것이 [-ㄴdaneun geosi] /-ndaneun geosi — An expression used to indicate that a result not intended during the preceding act appears in the following statement./
-ㄴ다는구나 -ㄴ다는구나 [-ㄴdaneunguna] /-ndaneunguna — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) An expression used to convey something newly heard to the listener with wonder./
-ㄴ다는군 -ㄴ다는군 [-ㄴdaneungun] /-ndaneun-gun — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) An expression used to convey something newly heard to the listener with wonder./
-ㄴ다는데 -ㄴ다는데 [-ㄴdaneunde] /-ndaneunde — An expression used to convey something heard or quote someone, adding the speaker's own thought or question./-ndaneunde — (informal addressee-lowering) An expression used to give the speaker's opinion indirectly, by conveying what the speaker heard./
-ㄴ다는데도 -ㄴ다는데도 [-ㄴdaneundedo] /-ndaneundedo — An expression used to convey what the speaker heard or someone's opinion, implying that there is a contradictory situation later in the sentence./
-ㄴ다는데야 -ㄴ다는데야 [-ㄴdaneundeya] /-ndaneundeya — An expression used to indicate that, based on what the speaker heard, the speaker had to say the following thing under the circumstances./
-ㄴ다는데요 -ㄴ다는데요 [-ㄴdaneundeyo] /-ndaneundeyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to give the speaker's opinion indirectly by conveying what the speaker heard./
-ㄴ다니 -ㄴ다니 [-ㄴdani] /-ndani — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when asking again or admiring an unexpected fact in surprise./-ndani — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to ask a question about a certain fact that is assumed to be already given./-ndani — An expression used to indicate that the speaker is surprised or impressed by what another person explained or stated./-ndani — An expression used to indicate that someone's explanation or statement is the basis for the speaker's judgment./-ndani — (informal addressee-lowering) An expression used to ask the listener about what another person said./
-ㄴ다니까 -ㄴ다니까 [-ㄴdanikka] /-ndanikka — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when the speaker emphasizes his/her remark, while reconfirming what was said earlier./-ndanikka — An expression used to quote the speaker's or another person's remarks as the reason or basis for the following statement./
-ㄴ다니까는 -ㄴ다니까는 [-ㄴdanikkaneun] /-ndanikkaneun — (informal addressee-lowering) An expression used to stress the speaker's position or opinion, when the speaker is questioned or given some request repeatedly./-ndanikkaneun — An expression used to stress that the speaker is quoting the speaker's or another person's remarks as a reason or basis./
-ㄴ다니까요 -ㄴ다니까요 [-ㄴdanikkayo] /-ndanikkayo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used when the speaker emphasizes his/her remark while reconfirming what was said earlier./
-ㄴ다니깐 -ㄴ다니깐 [-ㄴdanikkan] /-ndanikkan — (informal addressee-lowering) An expression used to stress the speaker's position or opinion, when the speaker is questioned or given some request repeatedly./-ndanikkan — An expression used to stress that the speaker is quoting the speaker's or another person's remarks as a reason or basis./
-ㄴ다니요 -ㄴ다니요 [-ㄴdaniyo] /-ndaniyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to say that the speaker is surprised or impressed by something, because it is unexpected./
-ㄴ다더군 -ㄴ다더군 [-ㄴdadeogun] /-ndadeogun — (informal addressee-lowering) An expression used to convey a newly learned fact to the listener./
-ㄴ다더군요 -ㄴ다더군요 [-ㄴdadeogunyo] /-ndadeogunyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to convey a newly learned fact to the listener./
-ㄴ다더냐 -ㄴ다더냐 [-ㄴdadeonya] /-ndadeonya — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) An expression used to question what is being stated, hinting strongly that the opposite is true./-ndadeonya — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) An expression used to ask the listener about what the listener saw or heard./
-ㄴ다더니 -ㄴ다더니 [-ㄴdadeoni] /-ndadeoni — An expression used to mention something the speaker knows from having heard it and connect this to the following content./-ndadeoni — An expression used to quote and confirm a well-known maxim, etc./
-ㄴ다더라 -ㄴ다더라 [-ㄴdadeora] /-ndadeora — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) An expression used when the speaker conveys what he/she heard to the listener./
-ㄴ다더라고 -ㄴ다더라고 [-ㄴdadeorago] /-ndadeorago — (informal addressee-lowering) An expression used to say something the speaker remembers from having heard it in person./
-ㄴ다던 -ㄴ다던 [-ㄴdadeon] /-ndadeon — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) An expression used to ask the listener about what the speaker thinks the listener knows./-ndadeon — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) An expression used to strongly deny, repulse, or question the preceding content./-ndadeon — An expression used to remember what the speaker heard in the past and modify the following content./
-ㄴ다던가 -ㄴ다던가 [-ㄴdadeonga] /-ndadeonga — (formal, moderately addressee-lowering) An expression used to ask the listener something to check the information./-ndadeonga — An expression used to talk to oneself or try to remember, while looking back on the past./
-ㄴ다던데 -ㄴ다던데 [-ㄴdadeonde] /-ndadeonde — An expression used to mention what the speaker heard from another person and relate it to the following content./-ndadeonde — (informal addressee-lowering) An expression used to indirectly express what the speaker wants to say, by conveying what the speaker heard, in an unfinished sentence./
-ㄴ다던데요 -ㄴ다던데요 [-ㄴdadeondeyo] /-ndadeondeyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to  give the speaker's opinion indirectly, by conveying what the speaker heard, in an unfinished sentence./
-ㄴ다데 -ㄴ다데 [-ㄴdade] /-ndade — (formal, moderately addressee-lowering) An expression used to repeat what the speaker heard, as it is./
-ㄴ다든가 -ㄴ다든가 [-ㄴdadeunga] /-ndadeun-ga — An expression used to choose one out of two choices./-ndadeun-ga — An expression used to indicate that it does not matter whether one chooses any of many choices./
-ㄴ다든지 -ㄴ다든지 [-ㄴdadeunji] /-ndadeunji — An expression used to choose one out of two choices./-ndadeunji — An expression used to indicate that it does not matter whether one chooses any of the many choices available./
-ㄴ다디 -ㄴ다디 [-ㄴdadi] /-ndadi — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) An expression used to ask the listener what another person said./
-ㄴ다마는 -ㄴ다마는 [-ㄴdamaneun] /-ndamaneun — A connective ending used when recognizing the truth of the preceding statement and adding something that is contrary or irrelevant to it./
-ㄴ다만 -ㄴ다만 [-ㄴdaman] /-ndaman — A connective ending used when recognizing the truth of the preceding statement and adding something that is contrary or irrelevant to it./
-ㄴ다며 -ㄴ다며 [-ㄴdamyeo] /-ndamyeo — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when the speaker confirms and asks questions about a fact that he/she heard./-ndamyeo — An expression used to ask if the current situation is different from what the listener or another person said before./-ndamyeo — An expression used to indicate that another person was saying something, while doing a certain act at the same time./
-ㄴ다면 -ㄴ다면 [-ㄴdamyeon] /-ndamyeon — A connective ending used when assuming a certain fact or situation./-ndamyeon — An expression used to assume that someone will express his/her thought or will, which becomes the condition for the following statement./
-ㄴ다면서 -ㄴ다면서 [-ㄴdamyeonseo] /-ndamyeonseo — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when the speaker confirms and asks questions about a fact that he/she heard./-ndamyeonseo — An expression used to ask if the current situation is different from what the listener or another person said before./-ndamyeonseo — An expression used to indicate that another person was saying something, while doing a certain act at the same time./
