-ㄴ데도 불구하고 -ㄴ데도 불구하고 [-ㄴdedo bulguhago] /-ndedo bulguhago — An expression used to indicate that the following content is different from or the opposite of what was expected in the  preceding statement./
-ㄴ데야 -ㄴ데야 [-ㄴdeya] /-ndeya — An expression used to indicate that because of the preceding situation, the speaker has no choice but to accept the following one./
-ㄴ데요 -ㄴ데요 [-ㄴdeyo] /-ndeyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to tell an unexpected fact with wonder./-ndeyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to demand an answer from the listener./-ndeyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to tell a situation, expecting a response from the listener./
-ㄴ들 -ㄴ들 [-ㄴdeul] /-ndeul — A connective ending used when a result is different from what was anticipated, even if that situation was a presumable outcome./
-ㄴ바 -ㄴ바 [-ㄴba] /-nba — A connective ending used to present in a prefatory manner a certain situation related to the following statement as a background or reason./-nba — A connective ending used to present a certain situation related to the fact as a background or reason in order to make a following statement./
-ㄴ지 -ㄴ지 [-ㄴji] /-nji — A connective ending used to indicate an ambiguous reason or judgment about the following statement./-nji — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to indicate a vague doubt./
-ㄴ지요 -ㄴ지요 [-ㄴjiyo] /-njiyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to ask a vague question./
-ㄹ -ㄹ [-ㄹ] /-l — An ending of a word that makes the preceding statement function as an adnominal phrase./-l — An ending of a word that makes the preceding statement function as an adnominal phrase and refers to assumption, prearrangement, intention, possibility, etc./
-ㄹ 것 -ㄹ 것 [-ㄹ geos] /-l geot — An expression used to close a sentence by giving an order or instruction./-l geot — An expression used to enable a non-noun word to be used as a noun in the sentence or to be used in front of '이다' (be)./
-ㄹ 것 같다 -ㄹ 것 같다 [-ㄹ geos gatda] /-l geot gatda — An expression used to indicate that the statement is a guess./
-ㄹ 것이 아니라 -ㄹ 것이 아니라 [-ㄹ geosi anira] /-l geosi anira — An expression used to stress that the following act must be done, instead of the one mentioned in the preceding statement./
-ㄹ 나름이다 -ㄹ 나름이다 [-ㄹ nareumida] /-l nareumida — An expression used to indicate that something depends on how the act mentioned in the preceding statement is done./
-ㄹ 대로 -ㄹ 대로 [-ㄹ daero] /-l daero — An expression used to indicate that something is in a severe state./
-ㄹ 듯 -ㄹ 듯 [-ㄹ deus] /-l deut — An expression used to guess that something said might be similar to the preceding state or situation./-l deut — An expression used to indicate that something is in a state which could be one of two possibilities./
-ㄹ 듯싶다 -ㄹ 듯싶다 [-ㄹ deussipda] /-l deutsipda — An expression used to guess the content of the preceding statement./
-ㄹ 듯하다 -ㄹ 듯하다 [-ㄹ deushada] /-l deutada — An expression used to guess the content of the preceding statement./
-ㄹ 따름이다 -ㄹ 따름이다 [-ㄹ ttareumida] /-l ttareumida — An expression used to indicate that the state or situation mentioned in the preceding statement is the only option./
-ㄹ 때 -ㄹ 때 [-ㄹ ttae] /-l ttae — An expression used to indicate the duration, period, or occasion of a certain act or situation./
-ㄹ 리가 없다 -ㄹ 리가 없다 [-ㄹ riga eobsda] /-l riga eopda — An expression used to indicate that the speaker is certain that the content of the preceding statement has little chance of being true./
-ㄹ 리가 있다 -ㄹ 리가 있다 [-ㄹ riga issda] /-l riga itda — An expression used to indicate that the speaker is certain that the content of the preceding statement has no explanation nor could it possibly be true./
-ㄹ 만큼 -ㄹ 만큼 [-ㄹ mankeum] /-l mankeum — An expression used to indicate that the following content is proportional to the preceding content or of a similar level or quantity./
-ㄹ 만하다 -ㄹ 만하다 [-ㄹ manhada] /-l manhada — An expression used to express that the act mentioned in the preceding statement is worth trying./-l manhada — An expression used to express that the incident mentioned in the preceding statement is highly likely to happen./
-ㄹ 모양이다 -ㄹ 모양이다 [-ㄹ moyangida] /-l moyang-ida — An expression used to guess that something could happen or be in a certain state in the future, considering another fact or situation./
-ㄹ 바에 -ㄹ 바에 [-ㄹ bae] /-l bae — An expression used to indicate that the speaker will choose the following content, because the content of the preceding statement is less satisfactory./
-ㄹ 바에야 -ㄹ 바에야 [-ㄹ baeya] /-l baeya — An expression used to emphasize that the speaker will choose the following content, because the content of the preceding statement is less satisfactory./
-ㄹ 법하다 -ㄹ 법하다 [-ㄹ beobhada] /-l beophada — An expression used to judge that the situation mentioned in the preceding statement is likely to happen./
-ㄹ 뻔하다 -ㄹ 뻔하다 [-ㄹ ppeonhada] /-l ppeonhada — An expression used to express that the incident mentioned in the preceding statement almost occurred but ultimately did not occur./
-ㄹ 뿐만 아니라 -ㄹ 뿐만 아니라 [-ㄹ ppunman anira] /-l ppunman anira — An expression used to express that the following content is also applicable, as well as the preceding content./
-ㄹ 뿐이다 -ㄹ 뿐이다 [-ㄹ ppunida] /-l ppunida — An expression used to indicate that the state or situation mentioned in the preceding statement is the only option./
-ㄹ 수 없다 -ㄹ 수 없다 [-ㄹ su eobsda] /-l su eopda — An expression used to express that the incident that occurred in the preceding statement is impossible./
-ㄹ 수 있다 -ㄹ 수 있다 [-ㄹ su issda] /-l su itda — An expression used to indicate that one has an ability to do something./-l su itda — An expression used to indicate that an act or state is possible./
-ㄹ 수밖에 없다 -ㄹ 수밖에 없다 [-ㄹ subakke eobsda] /-l subakke eopda — An expression used to indicate that there is no other method or possibility other than that./
-ㄹ 줄 -ㄹ 줄 [-ㄹ jul] /-l jul — An expression used to indicate that one knows how to do a certain thing./-l jul — An expression used to indicate that one either knows or does not know a certain fact or state./
-ㄹ 테고 -ㄹ 테고 [-ㄹ tego] /-l tego — An expression used to guess or believe that something is in the situation or state mentioned in the preceding statement, and continue the sentence./
-ㄹ 테냐 -ㄹ 테냐 [-ㄹ tenya] /-l tenya — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) An expression used to ask the listener if he/she has the will to do a certain thing./
-ㄹ 테니 -ㄹ 테니 [-ㄹ teni] /-l teni — An expression used to indicate the speaker's intention to do the act mentioned in the preceding statement, as a condition for the following statement./-l teni — An expression used to indicate the speaker's strong guess as a condition for the following statement./
-ㄹ 테니까 -ㄹ 테니까 [-ㄹ tenikka] /-l tenikka — An expression used to indicate the speaker's intention to do the act mentioned in the preceding statement, as a condition for the following statement./-l tenikka — An expression used to indicate the speaker's strong guess as a condition for the following statement./
-ㄹ 테다 -ㄹ 테다 [-ㄹ teda] /-l teda — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) An expression used to indicate the speaker's strong will or plan to do a certain thing./
-ㄹ 테면 -ㄹ 테면 [-ㄹ temyeon] /-l temyeon — An expression used to indicate that the listener wants to do or has the strong intention to do a certain thing, as a condition for the following statement./
-ㄹ 테야 -ㄹ 테야 [-ㄹ teya] /-l teya — An expression used to indicate the speaker's strong will or plan to do a certain thing./-l teya — An expression used to ask for the listener's thoughts or intentions regarding a future incident./
-ㄹ 테지만 -ㄹ 테지만 [-ㄹ tejiman] /-l tejiman — An expression used to indicate that the speaker is certain of the preceding content, but that the following content could be different from the former./
-ㄹ 텐데 -ㄹ 텐데 [-ㄹ tende] /-l tende — An expression used to indicate the speaker's strong guess about the preceding statement and add the relevant content./
-ㄹ거나 -ㄹ거나 [-ㄹgeona] /-lgeona — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when the speaker asks himself/herself about his/her thoughts or asks for another person's opinion./
-ㄹ걸 -ㄹ걸 [-ㄹgeol] /-lgeol — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to indicate a guess or assumption./-lgeol — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to indicate a small sense of remorse for or regret about something./
-ㄹ걸요 -ㄹ걸요 [-ㄹgeolyo] /-lgeolyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to indicate that the content of the preceding statement is the speaker's opinion or guess./
-ㄹ게 -ㄹ게 [-ㄹge] /-lge — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when the speaker promises or notifies the listener that he/she will do something./
-ㄹ게요 -ㄹ게요 [-ㄹgeyo] /-lgeyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used when the speaker promises or notifies the listener that he/she will do something./
-ㄹ까 -ㄹ까 [-ㄹkka] /-l-kka — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to indicate a question or assumption about something./-l-kka — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to ask for the listener's opinion./
-ㄹ까 보다 -ㄹ까 보다 [-ㄹkka boda] /-l-kka boda — An expression used to indicate that the speaker has an intention to do the act mentioned in the preceding statement./-l-kka boda — An expression used to indicate that the speaker is worried or afraid that the situation mentioned in the preceding statement may happen./
-ㄹ까 싶다 -ㄹ까 싶다 [-ㄹkka sipda] /-l-kka sipda — An expression used to say that the speaker makes a vague guess about the content mentioned in the preceding statement, or is worried that the content might happen./-l-kka sipda — An expression used to indicate that the speaker has an unclear intention to do the act mentioned in the preceding statement./
-ㄹ까마는 -ㄹ까마는 [-ㄹkkamaneun] /-l-kkamaneun — An expression used to indicate that the speaker accepts the preceding statement, while possessing questions or doubts that ultimately do not affect the following statement./
-ㄹ까만 -ㄹ까만 [-ㄹkkaman] /-l-kkaman — An expression used to indicate that the speaker accepts the preceding statement, while possessing questions or doubts that ultimately do not affect the following statement./
-ㄹ까요 -ㄹ까요 [-ㄹkkayo] /-l-kkayo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used by the speaker to guess and ask about something not happening yet, or unknown./-l-kkayo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to ask for the listener's opinion or propose something./
-ㄹ꼬 -ㄹ꼬 [-ㄹkko] /-l-kko — (formal, highly addressee-lowering)(old-fashioned) A sentence-final ending used to indicate the speaker's question or assumption about a certain fact./-l-kko — (formal, highly addressee-lowering)(old-fashioned) A sentence-final ending used when the speaker asks himself/herself or another person about a certain fact./
-ㄹ는지 -ㄹ는지 [-ㄹneunji] /-lneunji — A connective ending used to ask if the preceding statement related to the following statement will happen actually./-lneunji — A sentence-final ending used to indicate the speaker's question or doubt about the possibility of the preceding statement actually happening./-lneunji — (formal, moderately addressee-lowering or informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to ask if something uncertain will happen actually./
-ㄹ라 -ㄹ라 [-ㄹra] /-lla — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when worrying that something will happen as said in the preceding statement./
-ㄹ라고 -ㄹ라고 [-ㄹrago] /-llago — A sentence-final ending referring to a denial or doubt about a certain fact./
-ㄹ라고요 -ㄹ라고요 [-ㄹragoyo] /-llagoyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to indicate that because there is little chance of realization, the speaker doubts or cannot believe the preceding fact./-llagoyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to indicate that the speaker accepts a certain fact or situation wholeheartedly./
-ㄹ라치면 -ㄹ라치면 [-ㄹrachimyeon] /-llachimyeon — A connective ending used when making the preceding statement into the premise of the following statement through a previous experience./
-ㄹ락 -ㄹ락 [-ㄹrag] /-llak — A connective ending used when someone or something is about to do or become something./
-ㄹ락 말락 하다 -ㄹ락 말락 하다 [-ㄹrag malrag hada] /-llak mallak hada — An expression used to indicate that something is about to happen or almost happening./
-ㄹ란다 -ㄹ란다 [-ㄹranda] /-llanda — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) An expression used to show the speaker's thoughts or will, indicating that the speaker will do the act./
-ㄹ랍니다 -ㄹ랍니다 [-ㄹrabnida] /-llamnida — (formal, highly addressee-raising) An expression used to notify the listener of what the speaker wants to say./
-ㄹ래 -ㄹ래 [-ㄹrae] /-llae — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to indicate the speaker's intention to do something in the future, or to ask for the listener's thoughts about that./
-ㄹ래요 -ㄹ래요 [-ㄹraeyo] /-llaeyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to indicate the speaker's intention to do something in the future, or to ask for the listener's thoughts about that./
-ㄹ망정 -ㄹ망정 [-ㄹmangjeong] /-lmangjung — A connective ending used when admitting to the preceding negative fact and adding another fact that is the opposite of it./-lmangjung — A connective ending used to express the assumption that a certain negative situation will occur and emphasize the following statement that is the opposite of it./
-ㄹ밖에 -ㄹ밖에 [-ㄹbakke] /-lbakke — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to indicate that no other way exists except for that./
-ㄹ뿐더러 -ㄹ뿐더러 [-ㄹppundeoreo] /-l-ppundeoreo — A connective ending used when another fact exists other than a certain fact./
-ㄹ세 -ㄹ세 [-ㄹse] /-lse — (formal, moderately addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to explain the speaker's thoughts to the listener./-lse — (formal, moderately addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when one realizes something anew and admires at it./
-ㄹ세라 -ㄹ세라 [-ㄹsera] /-lsera — A connective ending used when worrying about something possibly happening./-lsera — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when worrying about something possibly happening./-lsera — A sentence-final ending referring to admiration./
-ㄹ수록 -ㄹ수록 [-ㄹsurog] /-lsurok — A connective ending used to imply that as the extent of the preceding statement becomes larger, that of the following statement also changes accordingly./
-ㄹ지 -ㄹ지 [-ㄹji] /-lji — A connective ending used when having a doubt about a certain assumption and relating that to the fact or judgment of the following statement./-lji — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to indicate the speaker's vague doubt./
-ㄹ지라도 -ㄹ지라도 [-ㄹjirado] /-ljirado — A connective ending used when presenting or assuming a certain situation and adding a situation that is irrelevant or opposite to it./
-ㄹ지언정 -ㄹ지언정 [-ㄹjieonjeong] /-ljieonjeong — A connective ending used to admit to or assume the truth of the preceding statement and emphasize the following contradictory fact./
-ㄹ지요 -ㄹ지요 [-ㄹjiyo] /-ljiyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to indicate a vague suspicion or guess./-ljiyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to ask the listener something in a roundabout way or politely./
-ㅁ -ㅁ [-ㅁ] /m — An ending of a word that makes the preceding statement function as a noun./-m — A suffix that makes the word a noun./
-ㅁ 직하다 -ㅁ 직하다 [-ㅁ jighada] /-m jikada — An expression used to guess that the content of the preceding statement is highly likely./
-ㅁ세 -ㅁ세 [-ㅁse] /-mse — (formal, moderately addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to express the speaker's intention to do something willingly./
-ㅁ에도 -ㅁ에도 [-ㅁedo] /-medo — A connective ending used to indicate that an event anticipated by the preceding statement did not happen or the opposite happened./
-ㅁ직스럽- -ㅁ직스럽- [-ㅁjigseureob-] /-mjikseureop- — A suffix that means &quot;to deserve to do something&quot; and makes the word an adjective./
-ㅁ직하- -ㅁ직하- [-ㅁjigha-] /-mjika- — A suffix that means &quot;to deserve to do something&quot; and makes the word an adjective./
-ㅂ네 -ㅂ네 [-ㅂne] /-pne — A sentence-final ending used when one finds a certain fact displeasing and says something as if quoting someone./
-ㅂ니까 -ㅂ니까 [-ㅂnikka] /-pnikka — (formal, highly addressee-raising) A sentence-final ending used when the speaker asks the listener politely./
-ㅂ니다 -ㅂ니다 [-ㅂnida] /-pnida — (formal, highly addressee-raising) A sentence-final ending used to explain the present action, state, or fact politely./
-ㅂ디까 -ㅂ디까 [-ㅂdikka] /-pdikka — (formal, moderately addressee-raising) A sentence-final ending used when asking about an event that the listener experienced personally./
-ㅂ디다 -ㅂ디다 [-ㅂdida] /-pdida — (formal, moderately addressee-raising) A sentence-final ending used when the speaker conveys and notifies someone of an event he/she experienced personally./
-ㅂ시다 -ㅂ시다 [-ㅂsida] /-psida — (formal, moderately addressee-raising) A sentence-final ending used to express an intention of doing a certain action together./
-ㅂ죠 -ㅂ죠 [-ㅂjyo] /-pjo — (formal, highly addressee-raising) A sentence-final ending referring to an argument or question about a fact that the speaker strongly believes in./
-ㅂ지요 -ㅂ지요 [-ㅂjiyo] /-pjiyo — (formal, highly addressee-raising) A sentence-final ending referring to an argument or question about a fact that the speaker strongly believes in./
-가 -가 [-ga] /-ga — A suffix used to mean a person who has that family name./-ga — A suffix used to mean a person who does something professionally or a person who does something as a job./-ga — A suffix used to mean a person who is good at something./-ga — A suffix used to mean a person who has a lot of something./-ga — A suffix used to mean a person who has a certain characteristic./-ga — A suffix used to mean a family./-ga — A suffix used to mean a street or a district./-ga — A suffix used to mean a song./-ga — A suffix used to mean a price./
-가량 -가량 [-garyang] /-garyang — A suffix used to mean approximately./
-각 -각 [-gag] /-gak — A suffix used to mean a large and tall house./
-간 -간 [-gan] /-gan — A suffix used to mean &quot;during.&quot;/-gan — A suffix used to mean a place./
-감 -감 [-gam] /-gam — A suffix used to mean a feeling./
-개 -개 [-gae] /-gae — A suffix that means a simple tool for doing such a thing and makes the word a noun./-gae — A suffix that means a person who has a property of doing such a thing and makes the word a noun./
-객 -객 [-gaeg] /-gaek — A suffix used to mean a guest or a person./
-거나 -거나 [-geona] /-geona — A connective ending used when one of either the preceding statement or the following statement can be chosen./-geona — A connective ending used when even if one out of many things happens, it does not have any relation to the following statement./
-거니 -거니 [-geoni] /-geoni — (old-fashioned) A connective ending used when recognizing the truth of the preceding statement and indicating that it is the premise or condition of the following statement./-geoni — A connective ending used when two contrasting actions or states are repeated./-geoni — A connective ending used when assuming a certain fact and recognizing it./-geoni — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when recognizing the truth of a certain fact based on experience./
-거니 하다 -거니 하다 [-geoni hada] /-geoni hada — An expression used to indicate that the speaker is guessing and accepts a certain fact./
-거니와 -거니와 [-geoniwa] /-geoniwa — (old-fashioned) A connective ending used to recognize the truth of the preceding statement, while indicating there is another relevant fact./-geoniwa — (old-fashioned) A connective ending used to indicate that the preceding statement will happen soon, adding other relevant content./
-거든 -거든 [-geodeun] /-geodeun — A connective ending used to convey the meaning, &quot;if something is true or turns out to be true.&quot;/-geodeun — A connective ending used to indicate that the following statement is obviously true by citing the preceding statement as an example./-geodeun — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending referring to the reason, cause, or basis for the preceding statement that the speaker thought of./-geodeun — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when talking about the premise of the following content and implying that the story continues./
-거든요 -거든요 [-geodeunyo] /-geodeunnyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to indicate the speaker's reasoning or the basis for the preceding content./-geodeunnyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to express the premise of the following content, indicating that the story continues later./
-거들랑 -거들랑 [-geodeulrang] /-geodeullang — A connective ending used to mean &quot;if something is true or turns out to be true.&quot;/-geodeullang — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending referring to the reason, cause, or basis for the preceding statement that the speaker thought of./-geodeullang — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when talking about the premise of the following content and implying that the story continues./
-거라 -거라 [-geora] /-geora — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when ordering./
-거리 -거리 [-geori] /-geori — A suffix used to mean &quot;to belittle that person.&quot;/-geori — A suffix used to mean &quot;while reappearing at regular intervals.&quot;/
-거리다 -거리다 [-georida] /-georida — A suffix that means &quot;continuously being in that state&quot; and makes the word a verb./
-건 -건 [-geon] /-geon — A connective ending used when even if one out of many things happens, it does not have any relation to the following statement./
-건대 -건대 [-geondae] /-geondae — A connective ending used when the speaker implies in advance that he/she will confess his/her thoughts or hopes, etc., in the following statement./
-건마는 -건마는 [-geonmaneun] /-geonmaneun — A connective ending used when the actual result is different from the result that was anticipated from the preceding statement./
-건만 -건만 [-geonman] /-geonman — A connective ending used when the actual result is different from the result that was anticipated from the preceding statement./
-걸랑 -걸랑 [-geolrang] /-geollang — A connective ending used to convey the meaning, &quot;if something is true or turns out to be true.&quot;/-geollang — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending referring to the reason, cause, or basis for the preceding statement that the speaker thought of./-geollang — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when talking about the premise of the following content and implying that the story continues./
-것다 -것다 [-geosda] /-geotda — (formal, highly addressee-lowering)(old-fashioned) A sentence-final ending used to indicate that the speaker guesses or assures himself/herself about something being true, when judging from his/her own experience, or with logic./
-게 -게 [-ge] /-ge — A connective ending used when the preceding statement is the purpose, result, method,  amount, etc., of something mentioned in the following statement./-ge — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to guess what the following statement would be like, if the preceding statement is such, and ask about it./-ge — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to indicate the following content would obviously be realized if the preceding content were true, but actually it is not./-ge — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when asking the listener to try to guess and answer./-ge — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when presenting the reason for the preceding statement./-ge — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when asking about the other person's intentions./-ge — (formal, moderately addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when ordering a person who is younger or has close friendly ties with you./
-게 되다 -게 되다 [-ge doeda] /-ge doeda — An expression used to indicate that something will become the state or situation mentioned in the preceding statement./
-게 마련이다 -게 마련이다 [-ge maryeonida] /-ge maryeonida — An expression used to indicate that it is natural for something to occur or become a certain state./
-게 만들다 -게 만들다 [-ge mandeulda] /-ge mandeulda — An expression used to indicate that someone creates the state or situation as indicated in the preceding statement./
-게 생겼다 -게 생겼다 [-ge saenggyeossda] /-ge saengyeotta — An expression used to indicate that someone is facing the situation expressed by the preceding statement./
-게 하다 -게 하다 [-ge hada] /-ge hada — An expression used to order someone to do a certain thing or to make something work./-ge hada — An expression used to accept or allow a certain act of another person./
-게끔 -게끔 [-gekkeum] /-gekkeum — A connective ending used when emphasizing that the preceding statement is the purpose, result, method, amount, etc., of something mentioned in the following statement./
-게나 -게나 [-gena] /-gena — (formal, moderately addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when giving an order in a friendly manner./
-게요 -게요 [-geyo] /-geyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to guess what the following statement would be like, if the preceding statement is such, and ask about it./-geyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to indicate the following content would obviously be realized if the preceding content were true, but actually it is not./-geyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used when asking the listener to try to guess and answer./-geyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used when presenting the reason for the preceding statement./-geyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used when asking about the other person's intentions./
-겐 -겐 [-gen] /-gen — An abbreviated word for '-게는', which is the combination of the connective ending '-게' and the postpositional particle '는'./
-겠- -겠- [-gess-] /-get- — An ending of a word referring to a future event or assumption./-get- — An ending of a word referring to the speaker's will./-get- — An ending of a word referring to a possibility or ability./-get- — An ending of a word referring to an attitude of speaking indirectly./-get- — An ending of a word that conveys the meaning, &quot;if one considers or examines something, it will become a certain way.&quot;/
-결 -결 [-gyeol] /-gyeol — A suffix used to mean &quot;during.&quot;/
-경 -경 [-gyeong] /-gyeong — A suffix used to mean that time or a time close to that date./-gyeong — A suffix used to mean the approximate time that it takes to do something./-gyeong — A suffix used to mean a device used to see something./-gyeong — A suffix used to mean glasses./-gyeong — A suffix used to mean a mirror./
-계 -계 [-gye] /-gye — A suffix used to mean a system./-gye — A suffix used to mean a document./-gye — A suffix used to mean a unit into which administrative work or tasks are divided./-gye — A suffix used to mean a field or an area./-gye — A suffix used to mean a limit./-gye — A suffix used to mean a world./-gye — A suffix used to mean an instrument that measures the extent of something./
-고 -고 [-go] /-go — A connective ending used when listing more than two equal facts./-go — A connective ending used when the preceding statement and the following statement happen in order./-go — A connective ending used when an action or result of the preceding statement remains the same while the following action happens./-go — A connective ending used when listing words that have opposite meanings./-go — A connective ending used when emphasizing the meaning of an adjective by repeating it./-go — A connective ending used to connect '이다' (be), a verb and adjective with such auxiliary predicate element as '있다,' '싶다,' '말다' and '나다.'/-go — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending usually used when answering, omitting the question that should follow and finishing the sentence./-go — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when ordering gently, as if giving a feeling of having omitted another ordering phrase that should follow./-go — A suffix used to mean an amount or a sum./
-고 계시다 -고 계시다 [-go gyesida] /-go gyesida — (honorific) An expression used to indicate that the act mentioned in the preceding statement continues to occur./-go gyesida — (honorific) An expression used to indicate that the result of the act denoted in the preceding statement continues to exist./
-고 나다 -고 나다 [-go nada] /-go nada — An expression used to indicate that the act denoted in the preceding statement has finished./
-고 들다 -고 들다 [-go deulda] /-go deulda — An expression used to press someone to do the preceding act./
-고 말다 -고 말다 [-go malda] /-go malda — An expression used to state that it is regrettable that the act mentioned in the preceding statement finally occurred./-go malda — An expression used to indicate the strong will of the speaker to accomplish the goal denoted in the preceding statement./
-고 보다 -고 보다 [-go boda] /-go boda — An expression used to state that the speaker did the act mentioned in the preceding statement, then realized the fact in the following statement./
-고 싶다 -고 싶다 [-go sipda] /-go sipda — An expression used to state that the speaker wants to do the act mentioned in the preceding statement./
-고 싶어 하다 -고 싶어 하다 [-go sipeo hada] /-go sipeo hada — An expression used to indicate that the speaker wants to do an action or fall into the state mentioned in the preceding statement./
-고 있다 -고 있다 [-go issda] /-go itda — An expression used to state that the act mentioned in the preceding statement is continued./-go itda — An expression used to state that the result of the act mentioned in the preceding statement is continued./
-고 해서 -고 해서 [-go haeseo] /-go haeseo — An expression used to state that the content of the preceding statement is one of several reasons for doing the act mentioned in the following statement./-go haeseo — An expression used to list two or more of the reasons for doing the act mentioned in the following statement./
-고는 -고는 [-goneun] /-goneun — A connective ending used when the same event is repeated./goneun — A connective ending used when the preceding statement is the premise or condition of the following statement./
-고는 하다 -고는 하다 [-goneun hada] /-goneun hada — An expression used to indicate that the same situation repeats./
-고도 -고도 [-godo] /-godo — A connective ending used to express a fact or feeling, and add that there is an opposite or another characteristic in the following statement./
-고말고 -고말고 [-gomalgo] /-gomalgo — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to emphasize one's agreement or affirmation to the other person's question or statement./
-고말고요 -고말고요 [-gomalgoyo] /-gomalgoyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to emphasize one's agreement or affirmation to the other person's question or statement./
-고서 -고서 [-goseo] /-goseo — A connective ending used to emphasize that the preceding statement comes before the following statement, or the preceding statement caused the following incident./-goseo — A connective ending used when the preceding statement is the premise for the following statement./-goseo — A connective ending used when the preceding and following contents contrast with one another./
-고서는 -고서는 [-goseoneun] /-goseoneun — An expression used to emphasize that the preceding statement comes before the following statement, or the preceding statement caused the following incident./-goseoneun — An expression used to stress that the fact mentioned in the preceding statement is natural or inevitable as the premise of the following statement./-goseoneun — An expression used to stress the former fact out of two opposing facts./
-고서야 -고서야 [-goseoya] /-goseoya — An expression used to stress that the act or situation mentioned in the preceding statement finally leads to the situation mentioned in the following statement./-goseoya — An expression used to stress that when the preceding statement becomes the condition, the situation mentioned in the following statement can occur only with great difficulty though its occurrence may be impossible./
-고야 -고야 [-goya] /-goya — A connective ending used when emphasizing that a movement or action is finished or is realized after reaching the end./-goya — A connective ending used when emphasizing that the preceding statement is the condition for the following statement./
-고요 -고요 [-goyo] /-goyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to ask the listener a question./-goyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to ask something discussed already or known to both parties./-goyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to add a related situation after a question./-goyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used when omitting the following statement, while the speaker adds something to the preceding statement or keeps talking./
-고자 -고자 [-goja] /-goja — A connective ending used when the speaker has a certain purpose, intention, hope, etc./
