-은 -은 [-eun] /-eun — An ending of a word that makes the preceding word function as an adnominal phrase and indicates an event or action having occurred in the past./-eun — An ending of word  that makes the preceding word function as an adnominal phrase and implies that an action has been completed and its state continues./-eun — An ending of a word that makes the preceding word function as an adnominal phrase and refers to the present state./
-은 것 -은 것 [-eun geos] /-eun geot — An expression used to enable a non-noun word to be used as a noun in the sentence or to be used in front of '이다' (be)./
-은 것 같다 -은 것 같다 [-eun geos gatda] /-eun geot gatda — An expression used to indicate that the statement is a guess./
-은 결과 -은 결과 [-eun gyeolgwa] /-eun gyeolgwa — An expression used to indicate that a situation occurred as a result of doing a certain act mentioned in the preceding statement./
-은 김에 -은 김에 [-eun gime] /-eun gime — An expression used to indicate that one is doing a certain act, after the act mentioned in the preceding statement or while doing a similar behavior at the same time./
-은 나머지 -은 나머지 [-eun nameoji] /-eun nameoji — An expression used to indicate that something is the result of a certain act or state mentioned in the preceding statement./
-은 다음에 -은 다음에 [-eun daeume] /-eun da-eume — An expression used to indicate that the time being described takes places after the completion of a certain work or process mentioned in the preceding statement./
-은 다음에야 -은 다음에야 [-eun daeumeya] /-eun da-eumeya — An expression used to indicate that a certain work or act must be completed, before the next situation becomes possible./
-은 대로 -은 대로 [-eun daero] /-eun daero — An expression used to indicate that something is the same as a previous act or state mentioned in the preceding statement./-eun daero — An expression used to indicate that something is the same as the present state./
-은 대신에 -은 대신에 [-eun daesine] /-eun daesine — An expression used to indicate that the states and behaviors mentioned in the preceding statement and following statement are different or opposites./-eun daesine — An expression used to offer something similar to make up for a behavior or state mentioned in the preceding statement./
-은 데다가 -은 데다가 [-eun dedaga] /-eun dedaga — An expression used to indicate that a certain act or state has been added to its preceding act or state, making it worse or better./-eun dedaga — An expression used to indicate that another fact or situation exists in addition to the apparent ones./
-은 뒤에 -은 뒤에 [-eun dwie] /-eun dwie — An expression used to indicate that one does a certain act mentioned in the preceding statement and a while later, does another thing./
-은 듯 -은 듯 [-eun deus] /-eun deut — An expression used to guess that something said might be similar to the preceding state or situation./-eun deut — An expression used to indicate that something is in a state which can be either one or the other./
-은 듯싶다 -은 듯싶다 [-eun deussipda] /-eun deutsipda — An expression used to guess the content of the preceding statement./
-은 듯하다 -은 듯하다 [-eun deushada] /-eun deutada — An expression used to guess the content of the preceding statement./
-은 마당에 -은 마당에 [-eun madange] /-eun madang-e — An expression used to indicate that one is in a situation or position where something mentioned in the preceding statement has already occurred./
-은 만큼 -은 만큼 [-eun mankeum] /-eun mankeum — An expression used to indicate that the following content is proportional to the preceding content or of a similar level or quantity./-eun mankeum — An expression used to indicate that the preceding statement is the reason or basis for the following statement./
-은 모양이다 -은 모양이다 [-eun moyangida] /-eun moyang-ida — An expression used to guess that something is in progress or in a certain state, considering another fact or situation./
-은 반면에 -은 반면에 [-eun banmyeone] /-eun banmyeone — An expression used to indicate that the facts in the preceding statement and the following statement are opposites./
-은 법이다 -은 법이다 [-eun beobida] /-eun beobida — An expression used to indicate that the act or state mentioned in the preceding statement has been decided already or is reasonable./
-은 이상 -은 이상 [-eun isang] /-eun isang — An expression used to indicate that one must do something or accept a certain situation because the preceding statement has been already decided./
-은 적이 없다 -은 적이 없다 [-eun jeogi eobsda] /-eun jeogi eopda — An expression used to indicate that the act or state mentioned in the preceding statement never existed./
-은 적이 있다 -은 적이 있다 [-eun jeogi issda] /-eun jeogi itda — An expression used to indicate that the act or state mentioned in the preceding statement existed in the past./
-은 줄 -은 줄 [-eun jul] /-eun jul — An expression used to indicate that one either knows or does not know a certain fact or state./
-은 지 -은 지 [-eun ji] /-eun ji — An expression used to indicate that a certain time has passed since a certain act mentioned in the preceding statement is done./
-은 채로 -은 채로 [-eun chaero] /-eun chaero — An expression used to indicate that the state remains the same as when a certain act mentioned in the preceding statement occurred./
-은 척하다 -은 척하다 [-eun cheoghada] /-eun cheokada — An expression used to indicate that one pretends to do or to be something, which is not true./
-은 체하다 -은 체하다 [-eun chehada] /-eun chehada — An expression used to indicate that one pretends to do or to be something, which is not true./
-은 탓 -은 탓 [-eun tas] /-eun tat — An expression used to indicate that the preceding statement is the reason or cause for the following negative phenomenon./
-은 편이다 -은 편이다 [-eun pyeonida] /-eun pyeonida — An expression used to indicate that something has a certain tendency or classified as such, instead of being sure of it./
-은 후에 -은 후에 [-eun hue] /-eun hu-e — An expression used to indicate that one does a certain act mentioned in the preceding statement and a while later, does another thing./
-은가 -은가 [-eunga] /-eun-ga — (formal, moderately addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to ask about a present fact./-eun-ga — A sentence-final ending used when the speaker asks himself/herself or guesses./
-은가 보다 -은가 보다 [-eunga boda] /-eunga boda — An expression used to guess about a fact mentioned in the preceding statement./
-은감 -은감 [-eungam] /-eun-gam — (formal, moderately addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to gently refute a certain fact./
-은걸 -은걸 [-eungeol] /-eun-geol — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when the speaker says a newly learned fact with admiration./-eun-geol — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when explaining one's thought or argument, or giving a reason for it./
-은걸요 -은걸요 [-eungeolyo] /-eun-geolyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used when the speaker says a newly learned fact with admiration./-eun-geolyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used when explaining one's thought or argument, or giving a reason for it./
-은고 -은고 [-eungo] /-eun-go — (formal, highly addressee-lowering)(old-fashioned) A sentence-final ending used to politely ask about or criticize a fact of the present./-eun-go — (formal, highly addressee-lowering)(old-fashioned) A sentence-final ending used to ask oneself something or guess about something./
-은데 -은데 [-eunde] /-eunde — A connective ending used to talk in advance about a  situation to follow./-eunde — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to admire something while anticipating the listener's response./-eunde — A sentence-final ending used to ask the listener while anticipating his/her answer./
-은데도 -은데도 [-eundedo] /-eundedo — An expression used to indicate that the following situation occurs, regardless of the preceding situation./
-은데도 불구하고 -은데도 불구하고 [-eundedo bulguhago] /-eundedo bulguhago — An expression used to indicate that the following content is different from or opposite to what is expected in the preceding statement./
-은데야 -은데야 [-eundeya] /-eundeya — An expression used to indicate that because of the preceding situation, the speaker has no choice but to accept the following one./
-은데요 -은데요 [-eundeyo] /-eundeyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to tell an unexpected fact with wonder./-eundeyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to demand an answer to the listener./-eundeyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to tell a situation, expecting a response from the listener./
-은들 -은들 [-eundeul] /-eundeul — A connective ending used when a result is different from what was anticipated, even if that situation was a presumable outcome./
-은바 -은바 [-eunba] /-eunba — A connective ending used to present in a prefatory manner a certain situation related to the following statement as a background or reason./-eunba — A connective ending used to present a certain situation related to the fact as a background or reason in order to make a following statement./
-은지 -은지 [-eunji] /-eunji — A connective ending used to indicate an ambiguous reason or judgment about the following statement./-eunji — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to indicate a vague doubt./
-은지요 -은지요 [-eunjiyo] /-eunjiyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to ask a vague question./
-을 -을 [-eul] /eul — An ending of a word that makes the preceding statement function as an adnominal phrase./eul — An ending of a word that makes the preceding statement function as an adnominal phrase and indicates assumption, prearrangement, intention, possibility, etc./
-을 것 -을 것 [-eul geos] /-eul geot — An expression used to close a sentence by giving an order or instruction./-eul geot — An expression used to enable a non-noun word to be used as a noun in the sentence or to be used in front of '이다' (be)./
-을 것 같다 -을 것 같다 [-eul geos gatda] /-eul geot gatda — An expression used to indicate that the statement is a guess./
-을 것이 아니라 -을 것이 아니라 [-eul geosi anira] /-eul geosi anira — An expression used to stress that the following act must be done, instead of the one mentioned in the preceding statement./
-을 대로 -을 대로 [-eul daero] /-eul daero — An expression used to indicate that something is in a severe state./
-을 듯 -을 듯 [-eul deus] /-eul deut — An expression used to guess that something said might be similar to the preceding situation./-eul deut — An expression used to indicate that something is in a state which can be either one or the other./
-을 듯싶다 -을 듯싶다 [-eul deussipda] /-eul deutsipda — An expression used to guess the content of the preceding statement./
-을 듯하다 -을 듯하다 [-eul deushada] /-eul deutada — An expression used to guess the content of the preceding statement./
-을 따름이다 -을 따름이다 [-eul ttareumida] /-eul ttareumida — An expression used to indicate that the state or situation mentioned in the preceding statement is the only option./
-을 때 -을 때 [-eul ttae] /-eul ttae — An expression used to indicate the duration, period, or occasion of a certain act or situation./
-을 리가 없다 -을 리가 없다 [-eul riga eobsda] /-eul riga eopda — An expression used to indicate that the speaker is certain that the content of the preceding statement has no reason nor possibility./
-을 리가 있다 -을 리가 있다 [-eul riga issda] /-eul riga itda — An expression used to indicate that the speaker is certain that the content of the preceding statement has no reason nor possibility./
-을 만큼 -을 만큼 [-eul mankeum] /-eul mankeum — An expression used to indicate that the following content is proportional to the preceding content or of a similar level or quantity./
-을 만하다 -을 만하다 [-eul manhada] /-eul manhada — An expression used to express that the act mentioned in the preceding statement is worth trying./-eul manhada — An expression used to express that the incident mentioned in the preceding statement is highly likely to happen./
-을 모양이다 -을 모양이다 [-eul moyangida] /-eul moyang-ida — An expression used to guess that something could happen or be in a certain state in the future, considering another fact or situation./
-을 바에 -을 바에 [-eul bae] /-eul ba-e — An expression used to indicate that the speaker will choose the following content, because the content of the preceding statement is less satisfactory./
-을 바에야 -을 바에야 [-eul baeya] /-eul ba-eya — An expression used to emphasize that the speaker will choose the following content, because the content of the preceding statement is less satisfactory./
-을 법하다 -을 법하다 [-eul beobhada] /-eul beophada — An expression used to judge that the situation mentioned in the preceding statement is likely to happen./
-을 불문하고 -을 불문하고 [-eul bulmunhago] /eul bulmunhago — An expression used to indicate that the speaker does not mind whatever was indicated in the preceding statement./
-을 뻔하다 -을 뻔하다 [-eul ppeonhada] /-eul ppeonhada — An expression used to indicate that the incident mentioned in the preceding statement did not happen, but that it was close./-eul ppeonhada — An expression used to exaggerate a certain past state./
-을 뿐만 아니라 -을 뿐만 아니라 [-eul ppunman anira] /-eul ppunman anira — An expression used to indicate that the following content is also applied, as well as the preceding content./
-을 뿐이다 -을 뿐이다 [-eul ppunida] /-eul ppunida — An expression used to indicate that the state or situation mentioned in the preceding statement is the only option./
-을 수 없다 -을 수 없다 [-eul su eobsda] /-eul su eopda — An expression used to indicate that the incident of the preceding statement is impossible./
-을 수 있다 -을 수 있다 [-eul su issda] /-eul su itda — An expression used to indicate that one has an ability to do something./-eul su itda — An expression used to indicate that an act or state is possible./
-을 수밖에 없다 -을 수밖에 없다 [-eul subakke eobsda] /-eul subakke eopda — An expression used to indicate that there is no other method or possibility other than that./
-을 줄 -을 줄 [-eul jul] /-eul jul — An expression used to indicate that one knows how to do a certain thing./-eul jul — An expression used to indicate that one either knows or does not know a certain fact or state./
-을 테고 -을 테고 [-eul tego] /-eul tego — An expression used to guess or believe that something is in the situation or state mentioned in the preceding statement, and continue the sentence./
-을 테냐 -을 테냐 [-eul tenya] /-eul tenya — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) An expression used to ask the listener if he/she has the will to do a certain thing./
-을 테니 -을 테니 [-eul teni] /-eul teni — An expression used to indicate the speaker's intention to do the act mentioned in the preceding statement, as a condition for the following statement./-eul teni — An expression used to indicate the speaker's strong guess as a condition for the following statement./
-을 테니까 -을 테니까 [-eul tenikka] /-eul tenikka — An expression used to indicate the speaker's intention to do the act mentioned in the preceding statement, as a condition for the following statement./-eul tenikka — An expression used to indicate the speaker's strong guess as a condition for the following statement./
-을 테다 -을 테다 [-eul teda] /-eul teda — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) An expression used to indicate the speaker's strong will or plan to do a certain thing./
-을 테면 -을 테면 [-eul temyeon] /-eul temyeon — An expression used to indicate that the speaker wants to do or has a will to do a certain thing, as a condition for the following statement./
-을 테야 -을 테야 [-eul teya] /-eul teya — An expression used to indicate the speaker's strong will or plan to do a certain thing./-eul teya — An expression used to ask for the listener's thoughts or intentions regarding a future incident./
-을 테지만 -을 테지만 [-eul tejiman] /-eul tejiman — An expression used to indicate that the speaker is certain of the preceding content, but that the following content can be different from the former./
-을 텐데 -을 텐데 [-eul tende] /-eul tende — An expression used to indicate the speaker's strong guess about the preceding statement and add other relevant content./
-을거나 -을거나 [-eulgeona] /-eulgeona — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when the speaker asks himself/herself about his/her thoughts or asks for another person's opinion./
-을걸 -을걸 [-eulgeol] /-eulgeol — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to indicate a guess or assumption./-eulgeol — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to indicate a small sense of remorse for or regret about something./
-을걸요 -을걸요 [-eulgeolyo] /-eulgeolyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to indicate that the content of the preceding statement is the speaker's opinion or guess./
-을게 -을게 [-eulge] /-eulge — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when the speaker promises or notifies the listener that he/she will do something./
-을게요 -을게요 [-eulgeyo] /-eulgeyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used used when the speaker promises or notifies the listener that he/she will do something./
-을까 -을까 [-eulkka] /-eulkka — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to indicate a question or assumption about something./-eulkka — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to ask for the listener's opinion./
-을까 보다 -을까 보다 [-eulkka boda] /-eulkka boda — An expression used to indicate that the speaker has an intention to do the act mentioned in the preceding statement./-eulkka boda — An expression used to indicate that the speaker is worried or afraid that the situation mentioned in the preceding statement may happen./
-을까 싶다 -을까 싶다 [-eulkka sipda] /-eulkka sipda — An expression used to indicate that the speaker is guessing the content indicated in the preceding statement vaguely or worried that the content can really happen./-eulkka sipda — An expression used to indicate that the speaker has an intention to do the act indicated in the preceding statement./
-을까마는 -을까마는 [-eulkkamaneun] /-eulkkamaneun — An expression used to indicate that the speaker accepts the preceding statement while possessing questions or doubts that ultimately do not affect the following statement./
-을까만 -을까만 [-eulkkaman] /-eulkkaman — An expression used to indicate that the speaker accepts the preceding statement while possessing questions or doubts that ultimately do not affect the following statement./
-을까요 -을까요 [-eulkkayo] /-eulkkayo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used by the speaker to guess and ask about something not happening yet, or unknown./-eulkkayo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to ask for the listener's opinion or propose something./
-을꼬 -을꼬 [-eulkko] /-eulkko — (formal, highly addressee-lowering)(old-fashioned) A sentence-final ending used to indicate the speaker's question or assumption about a certain fact./-eulkko — (formal, highly addressee-lowering)(old-fashioned) A sentence-final ending used when the speaker asks himself/herself or another person about a certain fact./
-을는지 -을는지 [-eulneunji] /-eulneunji — A connective ending used to ask if the preceding statement related to the following statement will happen actually./-eulneunji — A sentence-final ending used to indicate the speaker's question or doubt about the possibility of the preceding statement actually happening./-eulneunji — (formal, moderately addressee-lowering or informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to ask if something uncertain will happen actually./
-을라 -을라 [-eulra] /-eulla — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when worrying that something will happen as said in the preceding statement./
-을라고 -을라고 [-eulrago] /-eullago — A sentence-final ending referring to a denial or doubt about a certain fact./
-을라고요 -을라고요 [-eulragoyo] /-eullagoyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to indicate that because there is little chance of realization, the speaker doubts or cannot believe the preceding fact./-eullagoyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to indicate that the speaker accepts a certain fact or situation wholeheartedly./
-을라치면 -을라치면 [-eulrachimyeon] /-eullachimyeon — A connective ending used when making the preceding statement into the premise of the following statement through a previous experience./
-을락 -을락 [-eulrag] /-eullak — A connective ending used to show the manner in which someone or something is about to do or become something./
-을락 말락 하다 -을락 말락 하다 [-eulrag malrag hada] /-eullak mallak hada — An expression used to indicate that something is about to happen  or almost happening./
-을란다 -을란다 [-eulranda] /-eullanda — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) An expression used to show the speaker's thoughts or will, indicating that the speaker will do the act./
-을랍니다 -을랍니다 [-eulrabnida] /-eullamnida — (formal, highly addressee-raising) An expression used to notify the listener of what the speaker intends to do./
-을래 -을래 [-eulrae] /-eullae — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to indicate the speaker's intention to do something in the future, or to ask for the listener's thoughts about that./
-을래요 -을래요 [-eulraeyo] /-eullaeyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to indicate the speaker's intention to do something in the future, or to ask for the listener's thoughts about that./
-을망정 -을망정 [-eulmangjeong] /-eulmangjeong — A connective ending used when recognizing the truth of the preceding negative fact, and adding another fact that is the opposite of it./-eulmangjeong — A connective ending used to express the assumption that a certain negative situation will occur, and emphasize the following statement that is the opposite of it./
-을밖에 -을밖에 [-eulbakke] /-eulbakke — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to indicate that no other way exists except for that./
-을뿐더러 -을뿐더러 [-eulppundeoreo] /-eul-ppundeoreo — A connective ending used to indicate that another fact exists other than a certain fact./
-을세 -을세 [-eulse] /-eulse — (formal, moderately addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to explain the speaker's thoughts to the listener./-eulse — (formal, moderately addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to indicate that one realizes something anew and admires it./
-을세라 -을세라 [-eulsera] /-eulsera — A connective ending used when worrying about something possibly happening./-eulsera — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used when concerned or worried about something possibly happening./-eulsera — A  sentence-final ending referring to admiration./
-을수록 -을수록 [-eulsurog] /-eulsurok — A connective ending used to imply that as the extent of the preceding statement becomes larger, that of the following statement also changes accordingly./
-을지 -을지 [-eulji] /-ulji — A connective ending used when having a vague doubt about a certain assumption and relating that to the fact or judgment of the following statement./-ulji — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to indicate the speaker's vague doubt./
-을지라도 -을지라도 [-euljirado] /-uljirado — A connective ending used when presenting or assuming a certain situation and adding a situation that is irrelevant or opposite to it./
-을지언정 -을지언정 [-euljieonjeong] /-euljieonjeong — A connective ending used to admit to or assume the truth of the preceding statement and emphasize the following contradictory fact./
-을지요 -을지요 [-euljiyo] /-euljiyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to indicate a vague suspicion or guess./-euljiyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to ask the listener something in a roundabout way or politely./
-음 -음 [-eum] /-eum — An ending of a word that makes the preceding statement function as a noun./-eum — A suffix that means &quot;to be that&quot; and makes the word a noun./
-음 직하다 -음 직하다 [-eum jighada] /-eum jikada — An expression used to guess that the content of the preceding statement is highly likely./
-음세 -음세 [-eumse] /-eumse — (formal, moderately addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to express the speaker's intention to do something willingly./
-음에도 -음에도 [-eumedo] /-eumedo — A connective ending used to indicate that an event anticipated by the preceding statement did not happen or the opposite happened./
-음직스럽- -음직스럽- [-eumjigseureob-] /-eumjikseureop- — A suffix that means &quot;to deserve to do something&quot; and makes the word an adjective./
-음직하- -음직하- [-eumjigha-] /-eumjika- — A suffix that means &quot;to deserve to do something&quot; and makes the word an adjective./
-읍시다 -읍시다 [-eubsida] /-eupsida — (formal, moderately addressee-raising) A sentence-final ending used to express an intention of doing a certain action together./
-이 -이 [-i] /-i — (formal, moderately addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to express a state or feeling./-i — A suffix that means &quot;such a thing&quot; and makes the word a noun./-i — A suffix that means &quot;a person, an object, or a task&quot; and makes the word a noun./-i — A suffix that means &quot;a person or an object&quot; and makes the word a noun./-i — A suffix used to mean &quot;people of that number.&quot;/-i — A suffix that means &quot;in such a way&quot; and makes the word an adverb./-i — A suffix used to mean &quot;all of those things being in such a way&quot;./
-이- -이- [-i-] /-i- — A suffix used to mean causation./-i- — A suffix used to mean passivity./-i- — A suffix that means causation and makes the word a verb./
-이다 -이다 [-ida] /-ida — A suffix used to make the word a verb./
-이라기보다 -이라기보다 [-iragiboda] /-iragiboda — An expression used to indicate that the following statement is more appropriate than the preceding statement, when expressing or judging a certain incident./
-이키- -이키- [-iki-] /-iki- — A suffix used to mean causation./
-인 -인 [-in] /-in — A suffix used to mean a person./
-일 -일 [-il] /-il — A suffix used to mean a date./
-자 -자 [-ja] /-ja — A connective ending used to indicate that the preceding action was completed and then the following action occurred successively/-ja — A connective ending used to indicate that the preceding content is the cause or motive of the following content./-ja — A connective ending used to indicate that something has two characteristics at the same time./-ja — A connective ending used to imply that however the action or state of the preceding statement came true, it is no good./-ja — A connective ending used when making the following statement on the basis of what one saw or heard./-ja — A connective ending used to indicate that the speaker cannot tolerate a certain situation however hard he/she tries to./-ja — A connective ending used to indicate that the speaker has a certain purpose, intention, hope, etc./-ja — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to indicate the suggestion to do a certain act together./-ja — A suffix used to mean a person who receives respect./-ja — A suffix used to mean a very small-sized element./-ja — A suffix used to mean a machine or a tool./-ja — A suffix used to mean a person./
-자거나 -자거나 [-jageona] /-jageona — An expression used to give examples of, or list some suggestions or recommendations./
-자거든 -자거든 [-jageodeun] /-jageodeun — An expression used to indicate that someone's suggestion or recommendation is the condition or basis for the following statement./-jageodeun — (informal addressee-lowering) An expression used to conclude a sentence by quoting someone's order or request./
-자거든요 -자거든요 [-jageodeunyo] /-jageodeunnyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to indicate that the speaker's own behavior is based on another person's suggestion./
-자고 -자고 [-jago] /-jago — An ending of a word used to indicate a purpose or intention./-jago — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to emphatically state the speaker's thoughts or argument to the listener./-jago — (informal addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to ask again about a fact that one heard and confirm it./-jago — An expression used to indirectly convey someone's recommendation or suggestion./
-자고 들다 -자고 들다 [-jago deulda] /-jago deulda — An expression used to indicate that one is very eager to do a certain act mentioned in the preceding statement./
-자고요 -자고요 [-jagoyo] /-jagoyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to repeat or stress the speaker's suggestion or opinion./-jagoyo — (informal addressee-raising) An expression used to deliver the listener's remark and ask the listener to confirm or deny the listener's remark./
-자기에 -자기에 [-jagie] /-jagie — An expression used to present someone's suggestion as the reason or basis for the following statement./
-자길래 -자길래 [-jagilrae] /-jagillae — An expression used to present someone's suggestion as the reason or basis for the following statement./
-자꾸나 -자꾸나 [-jakkuna] /-jakkuna — (formal, highly addressee-lowering) A sentence-final ending used to advise the listener in a friendly manner to do a certain act together./
