두께 두께 [dukke] /thickness — The degree of thickness of an object./
두뇌 두뇌 [dunoe] /brain — An organ in the head of a person or animal, which governs neurons./brain; head — Wisdom that enables one to understand the logic of things./brain; mind — A person who is highly intelligent./brain — A person or organization that has the function of overall command./
두다 두다 [duda] /put; place; lay — To put something in a certain place./put; leave — To put something or someone in a certain situation or state./leave — To leave or abandon something or someone without taking or bringing it or him/her./put; add — To put extra ingredients into a dish./put; stuff — To put cotton or feathers into a blanket or clothes./have at; accommodate — To make a person stay./set up; establish; encamp — To establish a military camp./set up; establish — To establish a position, group or organization./put value on; place value on; emphasis on; focus on — To treat something with emphasis and care./have; bear — To have something such as a thought./understand; consider — To understand someone's situation or circumstances in an open-minded manner./belong to; be affiliated with — To have something as an official job or affiliation./set — To set the goal or basis of a certain behavior./put toward; let toward; leave toward — To let something face toward a certain direction./keep; treasure; store — To store or keep something without using it./set aside; leave — To postpone something without working on it./have gap; space out — To have plenty of time or spatial gap between something./take — For a certain situation to continue for a certain period./hire; have — To employ and use a person./have — To have someone as a family member, relative or friend./talk about; discuss; think about; ponder over — To have something as the subject of a conversation or debate./choose over — To deny the former and affirm or choose the latter./play — To play the game of go or janggi, Korean chess, or to place or use their pieces./place; set — To distance oneself from the world or people without having a close relationship./leave — To leave something or someone in a certain state./duda — An auxiliary verb used when doing an action in the preceding statement and continuously maintaining its result./
두더지 두더지 [dudeoji] /mole — A rat-like animal which digs holes with its long mouth and strong feet, and eats bugs./
두둑 두둑 [dudug] /ridge — A small bank or hill forming the boundary between two rice fields or patches./rib — A raised row made by plowing a rice field or patch and then piling up the dug up soil along the furrow./
두둑이 두둑이 [dudugi] /quite thickly — With quite large thickness./a lot — In such large amounts that something is more than sufficient./
두둑하다 두둑하다 [dudughada] /quite thick — Fairly thick./plenty — Enough, plentiful, and not deficient./
두둔 두둔 [dudun] /taking sides — The act of taking sides or covering up a mistake./
두둔하다 두둔하다 [dudunhada] /take sides — To take sides or cover up a mistake./
두둥실 두둥실 [dudungsil] /lightly; gently — A word describing the state or movement of something floating or rising softly./
두드러기 두드러기 [dudeureogi] /hives; rash; urticaria — A type of skin disorder featuring red bumps either due to an intake of certain food or medicine, or due to a change in environment./
두드러지다 두드러지다 [dudeureojida] /stick out; protrude; be embossed — For the center of something to protrude./remarkable; prominent; conspicuous — To appear clearly in the outside./bulgy — Something looking swollen as its middle part protrudes./prominent; remarkable — Noticeably distinct./
두드리다 두드리다 [dudeurida] /knock; tap — To hit or beat something in succession, making a sound./thrash; strike — (slang) To beat or hit./strike; touch someone's heart — To impress or move someone deeply./beat wildly — To hit something carelessly or recklessly./guess — To do something roughly by guesswork./
두들기다 두들기다 [dudeulgida] /strike repeatedly; pound — To knock on or beat something hard, making a sound./strike; touch someone's heart — To impress or excite someone./
