부둣가 부둣가 [budusga] /wharfside; quayside — The vicinity of a wharf./
부둥켜안다 부둥켜안다 [budungkyeoanda] /embrace; hug — To hold someone tightly in one's arms./
부드럽다 부드럽다 [budeureobda] /soft; smooth — Silky, not rough or stiff, to the touch./tender — The texture of food being not tough or hard./fine — The powder of something being very small and soft./soft; gentle — One's personality, heart, attitude, etc., being kind and warm./smooth — The operation of a machine, etc., being not stiff./smooth; light — An alcoholic beverage being not strong so as to go down smoothly through the throat./
부득부득 부득부득 [budeugbudeug] /obstinately; stubbornly — With the attitude of persisting or importuning unreasonably over and over, in order to get one's own way./with a grinding sound; grindingly — A word imitating the sound or describing the motion of rubbing a hard, tough, or slippery thing repeatedly, or grinding it against another one./
부득불 부득불 [budeugbul] /inevitably; from necessity — Because one has no choice but to do something although one is reluctant./
부득이 부득이 [budeugi] /unavoidably; inevitably — With no other options despite one's reluctance./
부득이하다 부득이하다 [budeugihada] /unavoidable; inevitable — Having no other options despite one's reluctance./
부들거리다 부들거리다 [budeulgeorida] /tremble; quiver — For one's body to keep shaking with cold, fear, or anger; to keep shivering with strenuous movements./
부들대다 부들대다 [budeuldaeda] /tremble; quiver — For one's body to keep shaking with cold, fear, or anger; to keep shivering with strenuous movements./
부들부들 부들부들 [budeulbudeul] /tremblingly; quiveringly — In the state of keeping shivering with cold, fear, or anger./softly — In a state in which a feeling to the skin is very smooth./
부들부들하다 부들부들하다 [budeulbudeulhada] /tremble; shiver — For one's body to shake severely and continuously with cold, fear, or anger; to shake one's body severely and continuously./soft — A feeling to the skin being very smooth./
부듯하다 부듯하다 [budeushada] /full — Closely packed and thus bulging./filled with joy; overwhelmed with pride — Fully joyful or moved./
부등식 부등식 [budeungsig] /inequation — A mathematical statement that the value of the left side is not equal to the value of the right side./
부등호 부등호 [budeungho] /sign of inequality — A sign that indicates which number or value is the biggest among more than two numbers or values./
부디 부디 [budi] /please; kindly — I hope eagerly; if possible./
부딪다 부딪다 [budijda] /hit; bump; crash — For something to hit hard against another thing; to make this happen./encounter; run into; be faced with — To suffer or hear of an unexpected event or situation./
부딪치다 부딪치다 [budijchida] /hit; bump; crash — (emphasizing form) For something to hit hard against another./encounter; run into; be faced with — (emphasizing form) To experience or face firsthand an unexpected event or situation./meet; encounter — To meet another person's eyes or gaze./encounter; come across — To meet someone by chance./collide with; crash into — To be placed in a conflicting relationship with another due to a different opinion or thought./meet — To meet someone involved in a certain affair in order to solve a problem./
부딪히다 부딪히다 [budijhida] /be bumped into; be run against — To come to run against something or someone very strongly./be run against — To come to experience or encounter an unexpected incident or situation./
부뚜막 부뚜막 [buttumag] /buttumak — The flat area around a pot installed in a fireplace in the traditional Korean kitchen./
부라리다 부라리다 [burarida] /glare; look daggers — To goggle one's eyes in such a manner that one's eyes are wide open./
부락 부락 [burag] /village; hamlet — A community consisting of several houses, usually in a rural area./
부락민 부락민 [buragmin] /villager — A person who lives in a village./
부랑아 부랑아 [buranga] /juvenile vagrant; guttersnipe — A child who roams around without a place to stay or a caregiver./
부랑자 부랑자 [burangja] /vagrant — A person who roams around without a home or a job./
부랑하다 부랑하다 [buranghada] /wander about; lead a vagrant life — To roam around without a home or job./
부랴부랴 부랴부랴 [buryaburya] /hurriedly; hastily — In a great hurry./
부러 부러 [bureo] /deliberately; intentionally — With one's real purpose or intention hidden./
부러뜨리다 부러뜨리다 [bureotteurida] /break; fracture — To make a hard thing break by snapping it./break; fracture — To make something break by hitting it or bumping against it./
부러워하다 부러워하다 [bureowohada] /envy; covet; feel jealous — To want to have something or want to be like someone after seeing it or him/her./
부러지다 부러지다 [bureojida] /break; be broken; fracture — For a hard thing to snap and be folded or break into two./be clear; be perfect — To speak or behave clearly and exactly./
부럼 부럼 [bureom] /bureom — nut: A hard fruit such as peanuts, chestnuts, walnuts, etc., which one eats on the 15th day of the first month by the lunar calendar by crunching on the nut in order to ward off all the evils for a year./
부럽다 부럽다 [bureobda] /envious of — Desiring to achieve or possess the same kind of feat or thing that others have achieved or possess because they look good./
부력 부력 [buryeog] /buoyancy — The power to float in a liquid or gas./
부록 부록 [burog] /appendix; attachment — Data attached as reference material at the end of a main text./supplement — A small booklet or thing that comes with a newspaper, magazine or book./
