빠뜨리다 빠뜨리다 [ppatteurida] /drop; let fall into — To make someone or something fall into a deep place such as water and a pit./put; leave — To place someone in a certain state or situation./leave — To drop and lose something./omit; miss; leave out — To not get something that is necessary./
빠르다 빠르다 [ppareuda] /fast; quick — Taking a short time to accomplish certain actions./speedy; fast — The time taken to move from one place to another place being short./rapid — The time taken for something to be achieved being short./early — Something being ahead of another thing in time./too young; too early — More time being needed to do something./
빠른우편 빠른우편 [ppareunupyeon] /express mail — A mail delivered overnight after it is received by the post office./
빠져나가다 빠져나가다 [ppajyeonagada] /get out; escape — To go outside the limited environment or  boundary./
빠져나오다 빠져나오다 [ppajyeonaoda] /come out of; get out of — To come outside the limited environment or  boundary./
빠져들다 빠져들다 [ppajyeodeulda] /sink into — To fall deeply into sleep, a dream, etc./sink into — To go deeper into a certain state or situation, failing to get out of it./be absorbed into — To feel drawn to something and come to like it more./
빠지다 빠지다 [ppajida] /fall out; slip out — For something embedded or inserted into something else to come out./be left — For a certain amount of profit to be left over after work has been completed./be missing; be short — To fall short of the necessary amount or number./flow off; be gone — For a liquid, gas, smell, etc., that are inside of something to flow or leak out./come out; discolor — For stains or dye to be washed off./be left out; be missing — To be omitted from a list; for something that should be included to not be included./grow weak; be exhausted — For a spirit, energy, power, etc., to disappear./drop out; skip — To not participate in a certain meeting or event, or leave midway through it./fall out — For the bottom of a vessel, the sole of a shoe, etc., to come out./become thin; grow slim — To lose one's weight./digress; stray — To go another way, leaving a certain place, or talk about another thing, changing subjects./have a good shape — For one's appearance, figure, etc., to be well-balanced./be inferior to; be below — To fall behind others./sink down into; be thrown into — To fall into water, a pit, etc./fall into; get into — To be put in a difficult situation./be deceived; be cheated; fall into — To be taken in by a plausible remark or trick./lapse into — To fall into a deep sleep or state of coma, etc./be addicted to; be absorbed in; be crazy about — To be excessively engrossed in something./ppajida — An auxiliary adjective used to indicate that the speaker is displeased because the quality or state described by the preceding words is very deteriorating or dismal./
빠짐없이 빠짐없이 [ppajimeobsi] /everything; all — All without exception./
빠트리다 빠트리다 [ppateurida] /drop; let fall into — To make someone or something fall into a deep place such as water and a pit./put; leave — To place someone in a certain state or situation./leave — To drop and lose something./omit; miss; leave out — To not get something that is necessary./
빡빡 빡빡 [ppagppag] /scrapingly; with a scratching sound — A word imitating the sound or describing the movement of rubbing or scratching hard and repeatedly./roughly — A word imitating the sound or describing the motion of tearing off a piece of thin, durable paper, cloth, etc./in the manner of scrubbing — A word describing the motion of wiping, rubbing, or polishing something repeatedly so that it is glossy./close; short — In the state of having one's hair or beard cut or shaved very short./stubbornly; obstinately — In the manner of keeping insisting unreasonably./with a grinding sound; grindingly — A word imitating the sound or describing the motion of grinding hard on something such as one's teeth./
빡빡머리 빡빡머리 [ppagppagmeori] /shaved head; person with a shaved head — A head that has been shaved or a person with such a hairstyle./
빡빡하다 빡빡하다 [ppagppaghada] /thick; stiff — Not soft due to little moisture./tight — Tight because there is no time, money, etc., to spare./stiff — Rigid and inflexible./stiff — Something being hard to be moved because it is too tight./dense; crammed — So filled that it is hard to find an empty space./
빤질거리다 빤질거리다 [ppanjilgeorida] /glisten; be glossy; be greasy — For the surface of something to shine and look smooth./linger over one's work; shirk — To be very idle, not doing one's work hard./
빤질대다 빤질대다 [ppanjildaeda] /glisten; be glossy; be greasy — For the surface of something to shine and look smooth./linger over one's work; shirk — To be very idle, not doing one's work hard./
빤질빤질 빤질빤질 [ppanjilppanjil] /greasily; glossily; lustrously — In a state in which the surface of something is very smooth and glossy./bold-facedly; brazen-facedly — In the manner of being very brazen and  impervious to shame./lazily; slothfully — In the manner of being very lazy, not doing one's work hard./
빤질빤질하다 빤질빤질하다 [ppanjilppanjilhada] /linger over one's work; shirk — To be very idle, not doing one's work hard./greasy; glossy; lustrous — The surface of something being glossy and very smooth./brazen-faced; bold-faced; shameless — Very brazen and impervious to shame./
빤짝 빤짝 [ppanjjag] /in a flash — A word describing a small light appearing shortly and soon disappearing./soberly — A word describing one pulling oneself together instantly./suddenly — A word describing a thought suddenly striking one's mind./like a flash — A word describing an object, person, incident, etc., appearing shortly and soon disappearing./interestingly — A word describing a certain remark suddenly catching one's attention./suddenly — A word describing one suddenly opening one's closed eyes./without a wink — A word describing one staying up, not sleeping./
빤짝거리다 빤짝거리다 [ppanjjaggeorida] /sparkle — For a small light to repeatedly and briefly appear and disappear, or to make this happen./
빤짝대다 빤짝대다 [ppanjjagdaeda] /sparkle — For a small light to repeatedly and briefly appear and disappear, or to make this happen./
빤짝빤짝 빤짝빤짝 [ppanjjagppanjjag] /in a flash — A word describing a small light appearing shortly and soon disappearing continuously./soberly — A word describing one pulling oneself together repeatedly./suddenly — A word describing a thought suddenly and repeatedly striking one's mind./like a flash — A word describing an object, person, incident, etc., repeatedly disappearing./without a wink — A word describing many people staying up, or one staying up several nights in a row./
빤짝빤짝하다 빤짝빤짝하다 [ppanjjagppanjjaghada] /sparkle — For a small light to repeatedly and briefly appear and disappear, or to make this happen./sparkling — A small light repeatedly and briefly appearing, disappearing, and remaining bright./witty — Having the wit to instantly come up with brilliant or surprising ideas./
빤짝하다 빤짝하다 [ppanjjaghada] /be sober — To pull oneself together suddenly./hit; strike — For a thought to suddenly come to one's mind./appear and disappear quickly — For an object, person, incident, etc., to appear shortly and soon disappear or end./catch one's attention — For a certain remark to suddenly catch one's attention./be seen clearly — For something to be seen clearly for an instant./stay up — To stay up all night, not sleeping./sparkle — For a small light to appear and disappear for a short time, or to make this happen./
빤하다 빤하다 [ppanhada] /light; luminous — Slightly bright as a light shines in darkness./obvious; palpable — Something being so obvious that one does not need to think about it deeply./fixed; steady; intent — One's staring eyes being clear./
빤히 빤히 [ppanhi] /brightly — In the state of being slightly bright as a light shines in darkness./plainly; obviously — In a state in which the result or state of something is clear./fixedly; steadily; intently — In the manner of one's staring eyes being clear./
빨가벗다 빨가벗다 [ppalgabeosda] /get naked; strip — To take off all one's clothes and be naked./
빨간불 빨간불 [ppalganbul] /red light — A red light of a traffic signal indicating stop at a crossroads, crosswalk, etc./red light — (figurative) A sign or air indicating danger./
빨간색 빨간색 [ppalgansaeg] /red color — A red color like that of blood, or a ripe apple or red pepper./
빨강 빨강 [ppalgang] /red — A color like a ripe red pepper or blood./
빨갛다 빨갛다 [ppalgahda] /crimson — Brightly deep red like the color of blood or a ripe chili pepper./
빨개지다 빨개지다 [ppalgaejida] /turn red; blush; redden — For a color to become red./
빨갱이 빨갱이 [ppalgaengi] /Red; Commie — (slang) A communist./
빨다 빨다 [ppalda] /suck; puff on — To put one's mouth in front of something and draw it in./suck on — To melt something in one's mouth or lick it./wash; clean — To put clothes, etc., in water and rub them with one's hands, or remove dirt on them by using a washing machine./
빨대 빨대 [ppaldae] /straw — A slender tube used for sucking up water or drink./
빨랑빨랑 빨랑빨랑 [ppalrangppalrang] /quickly — In the manner of doing something repeatedly, very lightly, and fast./
빨래 빨래 [ppalrae] /washing; laundry — An act of washing dirty clothes, cloths, etc., in water./laundry — Something that must be washed or that have already been washed./
빨래방 빨래방 [ppalraebang] /laundromat; coin laundry — A place equipped with coin-operated washing machines./
빨래집게 빨래집게 [ppalraejibge] /clothes pin — A fastener for holding clothes to keep them from falling or being blown by the wind when one hangs them up on a clothes line./
빨래판 빨래판 [ppalraepan] /washboard — A flat wooden board with a number of thin furrows, where clothes are placed and rubbed./
빨래하다 빨래하다 [ppalraehada] /wash clothes — To wash dirty clothes, cloths, etc., in water./
빨랫감 빨랫감 [ppalraesgam] /laundry — A variety of clothes or linens to be washed./
빨랫방망이 빨랫방망이 [ppalraesbangmangi] /washing paddle — A long piece of wood used for hammering clothes to clean them./
빨랫비누 빨랫비누 [ppalraesbinu] /laundry soap — A bar of soap used for washing clothes./
빨랫줄 빨랫줄 [ppalraesjul] /clothes line; washing line — A rope or cord installed for hanging up laundered clothes, linens, etc. to dry them./
빨리 빨리 [ppalri] /quickly — In a short duration of time./
빨리다 빨리다 [ppalrida] /be sucked — For liquid or gas to go into one's mouth./be squeezed out of — To be deprived of something valuable./be sucked into; be absorbed — To be deeply attracted by something./be washed — For things such as clothes, bedclothes, and shoes to be rubbed in water so that dirt comes off./suckle; nurse — To put something in front of someone's mouth and make him/her suck at it./
빨리빨리 빨리빨리 [ppalrippalri] /quickly — In a very short duration of time./
빨리하다 빨리하다 [ppalrihada] /quicken; hasten; accelerate — To shorten the time it takes to do something./
빨빨 빨빨 [ppalppal] /freely; in the manner of running around — In the manner of going around busily./
빨빨거리다 빨빨거리다 [ppalppalgeorida] /go freely; run around — To go around busily./
빨빨대다 빨빨대다 [ppalppaldaeda] /go freely; run around — To go around busily./
빨아내다 빨아내다 [ppalanaeda] /suck out; draw out — To make liquid, gas, etc., come out by sucking it./
빨아들이다 빨아들이다 [ppaladeulida] /suck in; inhale; soak in — To draw in or absorb liquid, gas, etc./draw — (figurative) To attract someone's mind./
빨아먹다 빨아먹다 [ppalameogda] /suck — (figurative) To take someone's things./
빨아올리다 빨아올리다 [ppalaolrida] /suck up — To suck liquid, gas, etc., so that it comes up./
빨치산 빨치산 [ppalchisan] /partisan; guerrilla — A member of an irregular military troop that ambushes and conducts surprise raids on enemies./
빨판 빨판 [ppalpan] /sucker — An organ attached to the arms of animals like various species of octopus, used for clinging to another object./
빳빳이 빳빳이 [ppasppasi] /straightly — A word describing an object being straight and hard, with no part being bent./stiffly — A word describing an attitude or personality that does not give in./
빳빳하다 빳빳하다 [ppasppashada] /straight — An object being straight and hard, with no part being bent./stiff — Having an unyielding attitude or personality./
빵 빵 [ppang] /bread — Food made by baking or steaming fermented dough./bread — (figurative) Food needed for living./boom; bang — A word imitating the sound made when a balloon, bomb, etc., explodes suddenly./boom; bang — A word imitating the sound made when a small hole is made, or describing the scene./(no equivalent expression) — A word imitating the sound made when a ball, etc., is kicked strongly, or describing that scene./honk — A word imitating the sound made when a car, etc., honks./
빵가루 빵가루 [ppanggaru] /bread crumb — Small pieces of bread made by finely grinding dry bread./
빵빵 빵빵 [ppangppang] /boom boom; bang bang — A word imitating the sound made when a balloon, bomb, etc., explodes suddenly and repeatedly./(no equivalent expression) — A word imitating the sound made when small holes are made repeatedly, or describing that scene./(no equivalent expression) — A word imitating the sound made when balls, etc., are kicked strongly and repeatedly, or describing that scene./honk honk — A word imitating the sound made when a car, etc., honks repeatedly./
빵빵거리다 빵빵거리다 [ppangppanggeorida] /keep kicking hard — To keep kicking hard a ball, etc./pop; boom — For the sound of a balloon's popping out or bomb's blowing up to be made continuously; or to make such a sound continuously./honk — For the horn of a car, etc., to be honked continuously; or to make such a sound continuously./
빵빵대다 빵빵대다 [ppangppangdaeda] /kick hard — To keep kicking a ball, etc., hard./pop; boom — For the sound of a balloon's popping out or bomb's blowing up to be made continuously; or to make such a sound continuously./honk — For the horn of a car, etc., to be honked continuously; or to make such a sound continuously./
빵점 빵점 [ppangjeom] /zero point — A state of getting no points./zero point; being poor — (figurative) A state of being insufficient and terrible./
빵집 빵집 [ppangjib] /bakery — A shop that bakes and sells bread./
빻다 빻다 [ppahda] /grind; crush — To break something into small pieces or reduce it to powder by pounding it with a stick, etc./
빼곡하다 빼곡하다 [ppaegoghada] /dense — Full without any empty space./
빼곡히 빼곡히 [ppaegoghi] /densely — Fully without any empty space./
빼기 빼기 [ppaegi] /subtraction; minus — An act of subtracting one number from another./
빼꼼히 빼꼼히 [ppaekkomhi] /distinctly — In the state of having a small, clear hole or crack./a little; lightly — In the manner of opening the door, etc., very slightly and quietly./a little — In a state in which only a little bit of something is seen through a small hole or crack./
빼내다 빼내다 [ppaenaeda] /pick out; pull out; draw out — To make something stuck or inserted somewhere, come out./select; pick out — To sort out something from many./steal; pilfer — To take someone's things, etc., secretly./poach; hire someone away — To entice someone to leave a group./help someone get out of; liberate — To set someone free from restraint./
빼놓다 빼놓다 [ppaenohda] /leave pulled out; leave plucked out — To leave something stuck or embedded somewhere, pulled./exclude; put someone on one side; leave someone out — To deliberately leave out or exclude someone or something from a group./exhaust — To make someone lose his/her concentration, energy, etc./
빼다 빼다 [ppaeda] /take out; draw; pull out — To take out something that is inside or lodged in something else./subtract; remove; draw — To remove or take away a part from the whole./roll out; squeeze out — To roll something into a long shape./withdraw; get back — To withdraw savings or get back the deposit./vacate; move out — To vacate a leased residence like a rented home./drain; air out; release; draw out — To cause the air, water, wind, etc., contained in a place to come out./remove; get out — To remove a stain or smudge by washing or rinsing./relax; enervate — To cause power, energy, etc., in the body to disappear./lose; remove — To reduce fat, weight, etc./stretch out; stick out; crane — To stretch out one's neck or tongue./stretch out; hold; sustain — To speak or sing by stretching out the word or note./resemble; take after; be a spitting image of — To inherit exactly the same appearance, characteristics, etc./dress oneself up — To be dressed in clean and neat attire./make an affected pose; assume airs — To show a false behavior or attitude intentionally./evade; shirk — To be not willing to do something because one dislikes it or is afraid of it./sneak away; turn tail — (slang) To run away to avoid something or someone./
빼다(가) 박다 빼다(가) 박다 [ppaeda(ga) bagda] /pull out and drive — For an appearance or situation to be very similar./
빼닮다 빼닮다 [ppaedalmda] /be the image of; be a dead ringer for — To take after someone in appearance, personality, etc./
빼도 박도 못하다 빼도 박도 못하다 [ppaedo bagdo moshada] /be unable to pull out or drive — For things to go so wrong that there is nothing one can do about it./
빼돌리다 빼돌리다 [ppaedolrida] /pocket; hoard secretly — To take a person or thing out secretly and hide or send him/her or it to another place./
빼먹다 빼먹다 [ppaemeogda] /leave out; omit — To miss a remark, letter, etc., out./skip; cut — To not do something one has to do./steal — To pilfer and take someone's things./
빼물다 빼물다 [ppaemulda] /stick one's tongue out — To stick out one's tongue and hold it between one's teeth./pout one's mouth haughtily — To thrust out one's firm lips because one is angry or has a complaint./
빼빼 빼빼 [ppaeppae] /bag of bones; skinny person — (disparaging) A very thin person./gauntly — In the state of being very thin./
빼빼하다 빼빼하다 [ppaeppaehada] /skinny; gaunt; haggard — Very thin./
빼앗기다 빼앗기다 [ppaeasgida] /be taken away; be stolen; be snatched — For one's possession to be taken away by force or come to belong to another./be taken away; be stolen — For one's possession to be taken away by force or come to belong to another./be deprived; be seized; be confiscated — To lose a qualification or rights legally./be captivated; be enthralled — For one's mind or attention to be captivated by something./be taken away; be deprived — For one's fidelity or virginity, etc., to be roughly used or treated with contempt./
빼앗다 빼앗다 [ppaeasda] /take away; steal; snatch — To take away another's possession by force or make it one's own./take away; steal; snatch — To take possession of a task, time, qualification, etc., by force./deprive; seize; confiscate — To cause another to lose his/her qualification or rights legally./captivate; enthrall — To captivate one's mind or attention./take away; deprive — To roughly use a person's fidelity or virginity, etc., and treat him/her with contempt./
빼어나다 빼어나다 [ppaeeonada] /outstanding; remarkable — Noticeably great among many./
빽 빽 [ppaeg] /with a shriek — A word imitating a sudden, high, and screechy sound./closely; densely — In a state in which several people or things are close together, fitting into a small place./
빽빽 빽빽 [ppaegppaeg] /with a shriek — A word imitating a sudden, high, and screechy sound that repeats./
빽빽거리다 빽빽거리다 [ppaegppaeggeorida] /shriek — For a high and screechy sound to be made suddenly and repeatedly./
빽빽대다 빽빽대다 [ppaegppaegdaeda] /shriek — For a high and screechy sound to be made suddenly and repeatedly./
빽빽이 빽빽이 [ppaegppaegi] /densely; closely — In a very small interval with little space in between./
빽빽하다 빽빽하다 [ppaegppaeghada] /dense; packed with — Having a very small interval with little space in between./
뺄셈 뺄셈 [ppaelsem] /subtraction — An arithmetic operation of withdrawing one number or formula from another; or such an operation./
뺏기다 뺏기다 [ppaesgida] /be deprived of; have something taken away; be stripped of — For one's possession to be lost or owned by another person forcibly./be deprived of; be stripped of — For one's job, time, qualification, etc., to be taken by another person forcibly./be deprived of; be stripped of — For one's right or qualification to be legally lost./be absorbed in; be fascinated by — For one's mind or soul to be captivated by something./be deprived of; be defiled; be taken away — For one's chastity, virginal purity, etc., to be violated and run roughshod over./
뺏다 뺏다 [ppaesda] /snatch; deprive; strip — To eliminate or take someone's thing by force./intrude on someone's time — To take one's job, time, qualification, etc., forcibly./deprive; strip — To make someone lose his/her qualification or right by using legal means./engross; absorb — To captivate one's mind or soul./defile; violate — To violate and run roughshod over someone's chastity, virginal purity, etc./
뺑 뺑 [ppaeng] /round; around — In the manner of going around a certain circumference./dizzily; giddily — In the state of suddenly feeling dizzy./in a circle — In the state of encircling something of a certain circumference./
뺑덕어멈 뺑덕어멈 [ppaengdeogeomeom] /Ppaengdeogeomeom — Ppaengdeok's mother: One of the characters in a novel from the late Joseon era, titled &quot;Simcheongjeon&quot;, &quot;The Tale of Sim Cheong&quot;; she is the mean and talkative stepmother of Sim Cheong; she eventually squanders all of her husband's wealth and leaves him after Sim Cheong is sold as a virginal sacrificial offering to the sea./Ppaengdeogeomeom — (figurative) A mean, talkative, ugly woman./
뺑뺑이 뺑뺑이 [ppaengppaengi] /spinning wheel; being at random; drawing of lots — A device for determining a certain grade by throwing something like an arrow into a target on a circular panel with numbers, figures, etc. written on it./
