속에 뼈 있는 소리 속에 뼈 있는 소리 [soge ppyeo issneun sori] /a sound with a bone inside — A word that has a profound meaning./a sound with a bone inside — A word that has a malicious intention./
속에 얹히다 속에 얹히다 [soge eonjhida] /have something sit on one's inside — To feel uncomfortable because something weighs on one's mind./
속에 없는 말[소리] 속에 없는 말[소리] [soge eobsneun mal[sori]] /remarks(words) that are not in one's inside — A remark that is different from what is really on one's mind./
속에(서) 방망이가 치밀다 속에(서) 방망이가 치밀다 [soge(seo) bangmangiga chimilda] /have a bat surge up in one's inside — To be overwhelmed with resentment./
속옷 속옷 [sogos] /underwear — Clothes worn directly on top of the body inside the outer clothing./
속을 달래다 속을 달래다 [sogeul dalraeda] /soothe one's stomach — To relieve disgusting or uncomfortable feelings in one's stomach./
속을 쓰다 속을 쓰다 [sogeul sseuda] /use one's inside — To worry or concern./use one's inside — To care for someone or do him/her a favor./
속이 끓다 속이 끓다 [sogi kkeulhda] /One's inside boils — For strong emotion to surge up within one's mind due to anger or resentment./
속이 내려가다 속이 내려가다 [sogi naeryeogada] /One's inside goes down — For one's anger or sulking to be relieved./
속이 떨리다 속이 떨리다 [sogi tteolrida] /One's inside trembles — To be very scared and afraid at heart./
속이 빈 깡통이 소리만 요란하다 속이 빈 깡통이 소리만 요란하다 [sogi bin kkangtongi soriman yoranhada] /A can whose inside is empty makes a loud noise; Empty vessles make the greatest sound; The worst wheel of the cart always creaks most — Something that is hollow inside looks gaudy outside as if it is rich in content./
속이 시커멓다 속이 시커멓다 [sogi sikeomeohda] /have a pitch-black inside — To be worldly-wise and think in a sly and crafty manner./
속이 치밀다 속이 치밀다 [sogi chimilda] /One's inside surges up — To be upset and overwhelmed with resentment./
속이다 속이다 [sogida] /deceive — To persuade someone into believing a lie./lie — To lie about something to another person./
속인 속인 [sogin] /ordinary person; common people — A person who is ordinary./vulgar person — A person lacking decency./layman — A person who is not a monk in Buddhism./
속임수 속임수 [sogimsu] /trick; deception — An act or method of deceiving others./
속잎 속잎 [sogip] /inner leaves — The inner part of a plant where many leaves grow overlapped./new leaves — Leaves newly growing on a plant or tree./
속저고리 속저고리 [sogjeogori] /sokjeogori — inner jeogori: One of the jeogori, traditional Korean female upper garments, worn underneath the outer clothes./
속적삼 속적삼 [sogjeogsam] /sokjeoksam — inner jeoksam: An undershirt worn under jeoksam, an unlined summer jacket, or a jeogori, a traditional Korean upper garment./
속전속결 속전속결 [sogjeonsoggyeol] /lightning war — The act of beating the enemy by delivering a series of swift attacks in a fight or war without dragging out the battle./lightning war — (figurative) The act of finishing a task quickly./
속절없다 속절없다 [sogjeoleobsda] /hopeless — Having no method of doing something./
속절없이 속절없이 [sogjeoleobsi] /hopelessly — Without a method of doing something./
속죄 속죄 [sogjoe] /atonement; expiation — The act of regretting one's wrongdoing or asking for forgiveness by doing a good thing./Atonement — In Christianity, Jesus Christ's saving of humanity by being nailed to a cross to pay for the sins of humans./
속죄양 속죄양 [sogjoeyang] /scapegoat — A sheep or goat offered by Jews as a sacrifice during Yom Kippur./scapegoat; fall guy — (figurative) A person who is punished for another's sins or wrongdoing./
속죄하다 속죄하다 [sogjoehada] /atone for; expiate — To ask for forgiveness by regretting one's mistake or doing something good./Atonement of Christ — In Christianity, for Jesus Christ to atone for the sins of humanity in his death on the cross./
속주머니 속주머니 [sogjumeoni] /inside pocket — A pocket inside a clothing or on an underwear./
속출 속출 [sogchul] /continuous occurrence — The happening of one event or incident after another./
속출하다 속출하다 [sogchulhada] /occur continuously — For one event or incident after another to happen./
속치마 속치마 [sogchima] /underskirt — A skirt to wear inside a skirt./
속칭 속칭 [sogching] /common name; typical name — A word or name that people commonly call something./
속편 속편 [sogpyeon] /sequel; follow-up — A narrative that continues the story following that in a book or movie that already exists./sequel; follow-up — A narrative that continues the story following that in a book, soap opera, movie, etc., that already exists./
속하다 속하다 [soghada] /belong — To be related to something and be within a certain range./
속행 속행 [soghaeng] /prompt action — An act of doing something fast./fast walking; fast going — A car or person going fast./continuation; resumption — The act of doing continuously./
속행하다 속행하다 [soghaenghada] /take a prompt action — To do something fast./walk fast; go fast — For a car or person to go fast./continue; resume — For something to be done; to do something continuously./
속회 속회 [soghoe] /resumption of a meeting — The act of resuming a meeting that was temporarily suspended, or making something that way./
속히 속히 [soghi] /soon; quickly — Quite fast, in a hurry./
솎다 솎다 [sokkda] /weed out; thin out — To pick out from here and there so that something dense can become sparse./
손 손 [son] /hand — A body part connected at the end of the wrist that is used to touch or grab something./finger — The end of the human hand that is divided into five parts./manpower — A working person./work — The power, efforts or skills needed for a job./hands; reach — Controlling authority or influence./guest — A person who is visiting from somewhere else./guest; customer; patron — A person who visits a business such as an inn, restaurant, etc./evil spirit; ghost — A ghost believed to cause trouble to people, traveling in different directions according to the date./son — A bound noun that serves as a unit for counting mackerels or croakers in twos./descendant; posterity — Children born after one's generation./
손 안 대고 코 풀기 손 안 대고 코 풀기 [son an daego ko pulgi] /blow one's nose without using hands — To achieve something very easily without much effort./
손(을) 거치다 손(을) 거치다 [son(eul) geochida] /pass through someone's hands — To pass through someone./pass through someone's hands — For something to be taken care of with someone's efforts./
손(을) 치르다 손(을) 치르다 [son(eul) chireuda] /receive guests — To receive a lot of guests in a big family event./
손가락 손가락 [songarag] /finger — The end of the human hand that is divided into five parts./
손가락 안에 꼽히다[들다] 손가락 안에 꼽히다[들다] [songarag ane kkobhida[deulda]] /be counted with[included in] fingers — To be extraordinary as one of the few./
손가락 하나 까딱 않다 손가락 하나 까딱 않다 [songarag hana kkattag anhda] /not even budge a finger — To do nothing./
손가락 하나도 움직이지 못하다 손가락 하나도 움직이지 못하다 [songarag hanado umjigiji moshada] /cannot even budge a finger — To be unable to do anything because one is very sick or exhausted./
손가락에 꼽다 손가락에 꼽다 [songarage kkobda] /count something with fingers — To be very small in number to such an extent that can be counted on the fingers./
손가락으로 헤아릴 정도 손가락으로 헤아릴 정도 [songarageuro hearil jeongdo] /to such an extent that it can be counted with fingers — An expression used to refer to a very small number./
손가락을 꼽다 손가락을 꼽다 [songarageul kkobda] /count on one's fingers — To look forward to something or a day counting down the days left./
손가락을 빨다 손가락을 빨다 [songarageul ppalda] /suck one's fingers — To do nothing in an important or urgent situation./suck one's fingers — To starve with nothing to eat./
손가락질 손가락질 [songaragjil] /finger-pointing; pointing — The act of indicating something with a finger./criticism — The act of finding fault with others./
손가락질(을) 받다 손가락질(을) 받다 [songaragjil(eul) badda] /receive finger-pointing — To be laughted at or criticized by others./
손가락질하다 손가락질하다 [songaragjilhada] /point — To point at something with one's finger./badmouth — To speak unfavorably about another person./
손가방 손가방 [songabang] /handbag; purse — A small bag to carry in one's hand./
손거울 손거울 [songeoul] /hand mirror — A small mirror that one carries around to look into./
손금 손금 [songeum] /palm line — The creases on the skin of the palm./
손금(을) 보다 손금(을) 보다 [songeum(eul) boda] /look at someone's palm lines — To forecast someone's fortune or destiny by looking at the lines drawn in his/her palm./
손길 손길 [songil] /hand — A hand that is extended, held, brushed, or touched./hand; helping hand — (figurative) The act of helping out, or of doing harm./touch; skill — The movement of the hand./
손길을 뻗다 손길을 뻗다 [songileul ppeodda] /stretch out one's hands — To act intentionally in order to have an influence on something./
손길을 뻗치다 손길을 뻗치다 [songileul ppeodchida] /stretch out one's hands — To act aggressively and intentionally in order to have an influence on something./
손깍지 손깍지 [sonkkagji] /folding one's hands — The state in which two hands are held with their fingers intercrossed./
손꼽다 손꼽다 [sonkkobda] /count with one's fingers — To count numbers by closing the fingers one by one./be highly regarded; be eminent — To recognize with praise someone as being in the top five among a field of many./be very few; be rare — To be able to count with five fingers because the number is small./choose; select — To regard someone or something as the best among many./
손꼽아 기다리다 손꼽아 기다리다 [sonkkoba gidarida] /wait by counting with one's fingers — To look forward to something counting on one's fingers./
손꼽히다 손꼽히다 [sonkkobhida] /be considered great — To be considered as one of the best five among many./be very few — To be counted within the five fingers because the number is small./rank first — To be considered the best among many./
손끝 손끝 [sonkkeut] /fingertip — The tip of a finger./
손끝 하나 까딱 안 하다 손끝 하나 까딱 안 하다 [sonkkeut hana kkattag an hada] /not even budge a fingertip — To do nothing but idle away./
손끝 하나 못 대다 손끝 하나 못 대다 [sonkkeut hana mos daeda] /cannot touch something even with a fingertip — To be unable to engage in something or provoke someone./
손끝(이) 맵다 손끝(이) 맵다 [sonkkeut(i) maebda] /have a spicy fingertip — For one's slight hit to hurt very much./have a spicy fingertip — To work in a shrewd and seamless manner./
손끝(이) 야무지다 손끝(이) 야무지다 [sonkkeut(i) yamujida] /have dexterous fingertips — To work in a meticulous and seamless manner./
손끝(이) 여물다[야물다] 손끝(이) 여물다[야물다] [sonkkeut(i) yeomulda[yamulda]] /have ripe fingertips — To work in a meticulous and seamless manner./
손나팔 손나팔 [sonnapal] /blowing a hand trumpet — An act of putting one's hands to the mouth and making sounds as if one is blowing a trumpet./hands held up like a trumpet — Hands held up to one's mouth forming a trumpet-like shape when saying something loudly or quietly./
손날 손날 [sonnal] /edge of the hand — The part of an open hand between the end of the little finger and the wrist./
손녀 손녀 [sonnyeo] /granddaughter — The daughter of one's son or daughter./
손녀딸 손녀딸 [sonnyeottal] /granddaughter — (endearing) One's granddaughter./
손놀림 손놀림 [sonnolrim] /hand gestures — The act of moving one's hands this way and that./
손님 손님 [sonnim] /visitor; caller; guest — (honorific) A person who comes to visit someone./guest; customer — (honorific) A person who visits an inn, restaurant, etc./guest — A person who comes to attend a wedding, funeral, etc./visitor — A person who comes to see a performance, exhibition, etc./passenger — A person who uses a means of transportation such as a bus, taxi, etc./
손님맞이 손님맞이 [sonnimmaji] /reception of guests — The act of greeting one's guests./
손님상 손님상 [sonnimsang] /guest's meal — A meal prepared for a guest./
손님치레 손님치레 [sonnimchire] /reception of guests — The act of receiving guests and treating them well./
손대다 손대다 [sondaeda] /touch; make physical contact; lay one's hands on — To feel something with one's hand./set about; put one's hand to — To start something./touch; sport with — To do a sexual act./hit; give a blow; strike — To beat someone./amend; refine; touch up — To revise or polish./put one's hands on; touch — To steal or illegally use someone's property./touch; have a finger in — To get involved in something bad./control; deal with — To handle or manage someone or something at one's will./eat; touch — To eat food./
손도장 손도장 [sondojang] /thumbmark; thumbprint — The act of stamping one's thumb instead of a seal with red seal-ink./
손동작 손동작 [sondongjag] /hand gestures — Movements of the hand./
손들다 손들다 [sondeulda] /give up — To give up or abandon something in the middle of doing it because it is hard to handle by oneself./agree — To agree to a suggestion or opinion./raise — To raise one's hand above one's head./
손등 손등 [sondeung] /back of one's hand — The outside part of the hand; the opposite side of the palm./
손때 손때 [sonttae] /trace of one's touch — The trace of being used or touched for a long time, which allows the owner to feel comfortable using the item./finger stain — A smear formed on an item by laying hands on or touching it./hand stain — A stain smeared on the hand./
손때(가) 맵다 손때(가) 맵다 [sonttae(ga) maebda] /have a spicy trace of one's touch — For one's slight hit to hurt very much./have a spicy trace of one's touch — To work in a shrewd and seamless manner./
손때(가) 묻다[먹다] 손때(가) 묻다[먹다] [sonttae(ga) mudda[meogda]] /be stained with[get] traces of one's touch — For plates, furniture, etc., to become comfortable to use as they have been used for a long time./
손때가 오르다 손때가 오르다 [sonttaega oreuda] /The traces of one's touch accumulate — For plates, furniture, etc., to become comfortable to use as they have been used for a long time./
손마디 손마디 [sonmadi] /knuckle — The joints of one's fingers./
손맛 손맛 [sonmas] /touch; feel — The sensation one feels from touching something with a hand./sensation — The sensation that an angler feels when a fish takes the bait on his/her fishing rod./hand taste — Literally, the taste from a hand, meaning that the hands used in making food give a special flavor to the food./
손목 손목 [sonmog] /wrist — The part that connects the hand and the arm./
손목시계 손목시계 [sonmogsigye] /wristwatch — A small watch made to wear on the wrist./
손바닥 손바닥 [sonbadag] /palm — The inside of the hand as opposed to the back of the hand./
손바닥(을) 뒤집듯 손바닥(을) 뒤집듯 [sonbadag(eul) dwijibdeus] /as if flipping one's palm — Changing one's attitude suddenly and easily./as if flipping one's palm — To do something very easily./
손바닥에 장을 지지겠다 손바닥에 장을 지지겠다 [sonbadage jangeul jijigessda] /I will burn my palm — An expression used to guarantee that someone will not be able to achieve something./I will burn my palm — An expression used to guarantee that one's argument will turn out to be true./
손바닥으로 하늘 가리기 손바닥으로 하늘 가리기 [sonbadageuro haneul garigi] /covering the sky with one's hands — An expression used to describe a situation where something can hardly be concealed./
손바닥을 뒤집는 것처럼 쉽다 손바닥을 뒤집는 것처럼 쉽다 [sonbadageul dwijibneun geoscheoreom swibda] /be easy like flipping one's palm — To have little difficulty in doing something./
손바닥을 맞추다 손바닥을 맞추다 [sonbadageul majchuda] /put together eath other's palms — To be of one mind./
손발 손발 [sonbal] /hands and feet — Hands and feet/hands and feet — (figurative) A person that one can use like his/her hands and feet./
손발(이) 맞다 손발(이) 맞다 [sonbal(i) majda] /have hands and feet coordinate with each other — For each other's mind, opinion, action, etc., to harmonize when working together./
손발을 맞추다 손발을 맞추다 [sonbaleul majchuda] /coordinate each other's hands and feet — To harmonize each other's mind, opinion, action, etc., when working together./
손발이 닳도록 빌다 손발이 닳도록 빌다 [sonbali dalhdorog bilda] /beg to such an extent that one's hands and feet wear out — To desperately beg for forgiveness./
손발이 따로 놀다 손발이 따로 놀다 [sonbali ttaro nolda] /have one's hands and feet move separately — For each other's mind, opinion, action, etc., not to harmonize when working together./
손버릇 손버릇 [sonbeoreus] /hand habit — The habit of using one's hands in a certain way./stealing habit — The habit of stealing things from others./violent habit — The bad habit of breaking things or hitting others./
손버릇(이) 사납다 손버릇(이) 사납다 [sonbeoreus(i) sanabda] /have a ferocious hand habit — To have a habit of stealing or destroying others' possessions or beating others./
손보다 손보다 [sonboda] /fix — To mend and repair well so that there is no flaw./scold — (slang) To punish or to hit as a punishment./
손빨래 손빨래 [sonppalrae] /hand-washing — Laundry done with one's hands./
손뼉 손뼉 [sonppyeog] /palm — The entire palm of the hand./
손뼉(을) 치다 손뼉(을) 치다 [sonppyeog(eul) chida] /clap one's palms — To agree on or be happy with something./
손사래 손사래 [sonsarae] /waving one's hand — The act of opening one's hands and waving them when denying or refusing something./
손상 손상 [sonsang] /damage; harm; impairment — A state in which a certain object has been broken or harmed./injury — A state in which someone is sick or a part of his/her body has been hurt./deterioration; impairment — The worsening of the quality of something./degradation; injury — A state in which one's honor, dignity, value, etc., has been damaged./
손상되다 손상되다 [sonsangdoeda] /be damaged; be harmed — For an object to be broken or impaired./be injured; be harmed; be impaired — To fall sick; for a part of one's body to be hurt./be damaged — For a quality to become worse./be harmed; be hurt — For one's honor, dignity, value, etc., to be lost./
손상하다 손상하다 [sonsanghada] /damage; injure; impair — To break or hurt a certain object./injure; harm — To make someone sick or make a part of his/her body hurt./harm — To worsen the quality, etc., of something./harm; injure — To damage one's honor, dignity, value, etc./
손색 손색 [sonsaeg] /inferiority — Weaknesses when compared to another person or thing./
손색없다 손색없다 [sonsaegeobsda] /not inferior — Having no weaknesses when compared to another person or thing./
손색없이 손색없이 [sonsaegeobsi] /as good as others — Without weaknesses when compared to another person or thing./
손세탁 손세탁 [sonsetag] /hand-washing — The act of washing clothes, fabric, etc., with one's hands./
손세탁하다 손세탁하다 [sonsetaghada] /hand-wash — To wash clothes, fabric, etc., with one's hands./
손수 손수 [sonsu] /with one's own hands; oneself — In person without receiving other's help./
손수건 손수건 [sonsugeon] /handkerchief — A small, thin towel that one carries to use./
손수레 손수레 [sonsure] /handcart — A cart that a person draws with his/her hands./
손쉽다 손쉽다 [sonswibda] /easy — Not difficult to deal with or handle./
손실 손실 [sonsil] /loss; damage — The state of suffering a loss due to something reduced or lost./
손실되다 손실되다 [sonsildoeda] /be lost — To suffer a loss due to something reduced or lost./
손실액 손실액 [sonsilaeg] /loss — The amount of money lost due to something reduced or lost./
손실하다 손실하다 [sonsilhada] /lose — To suffer a loss due to something reduced or lost./
손쓰다 손쓰다 [sonsseuda] /take an action — To take the necessary measure to solve or prepare for something./
손아귀 손아귀 [sonagwi] /grip; clutch — The part between the thumb and the rest four fingers./grip; clutch — The power of gripping something./hands — The range of power of influence./
손아귀에 넣다 손아귀에 넣다 [sonagwie neohda] /get something in one's hands; get something in one's grip — To make something come under one's power or influence./
손아래 손아래 [sonarae] /being younger; being subordinate — A relationship in which the other person is lower in age, status, etc., or a person in such a relationship./
손아래뻘 손아래뻘 [sonaraeppeol] /being younger — A word that shows that a person is lower in age, status, etc., than oneself./
손아랫사람 손아랫사람 [sonaraessaram] /junior; inferior — A person who is lower in age, status, etc., than oneself./
손안 손안 [sonan] /palm; grip — The inside of the hand./hands — The range where one can take control or exert influence./
손안에 넣다 손안에 넣다 [sonane neohda] /get something in one's hands; get something in one's grip — To make something come under one's power or influence./
손안에 놓인 듯 손안에 놓인 듯 [sonane nohin deus] /as if being put in one's hands — Clearly as if something is located very closely./
손안에서 주무르다 손안에서 주무르다 [sonaneseo jumureuda] /knead something or someone in one's hands — To control something or someone as one pleases./
손에 걸리다 손에 걸리다 [sone geolrida] /be caught in someone's hands — To be caught by someone./
손에 땀을 쥐다 손에 땀을 쥐다 [sone ttameul jwida] /hold sweat in one's hands — To feel very nervous due to the tension of a situation./
손에 붙다 손에 붙다 [sone butda] /have something attached to one's hands — To get accustomed to something and deal with it in a more enthusiastic and efficient manner./
손에 익다 손에 익다 [sone igda] /have one's hands get accustomed to something — To get accustomed to something./
손에 잡히다 손에 잡히다 [sone jabhida] /grab something in one's hands — To work in an enthusiastic and efficient manner as one solved a problem or recovered one's composure./
손에 잡힐 듯하다 손에 잡힐 듯하다 [sone jabhil deushada] /as if something can be grabbed in one's hands — For something to be seen very clear and near./
손에 장을 지지다 손에 장을 지지다 [sone jangeul jijida] /burn one's hand; eat one's hat — To stress that something will never happen or one will never do something./
손위 손위 [sonwi] /being older — A relationship in which the other person is higher in age, status, etc., or a person in such a relationship./
손윗사람 손윗사람 [sonwissaram] /elder; senior — A person who is higher in age, status, etc., than oneself./
손을 끊다 손을 끊다 [soneul kkeunhda] /cut off one's hands — To no longer have a relationhip with someone./
손을 나누다 손을 나누다 [soneul nanuda] /divide work — For many people to divide up work./
손을 내밀다 손을 내밀다 [soneul naemilda] /extend one's hands — To ask for help./extend one's hands — To make the first move to get close to someone./
손을 놓다 손을 놓다 [soneul nohda] /put down one's hands — To quit or briefly stop something that one has been working on./
손을 떼다[털다] 손을 떼다[털다] [soneul tteda[teolda]] /take[dust] one's hands off — To quit something that one has been working on./
손을 맞잡다 손을 맞잡다 [soneul majjabda] /hold each other's hands — To closely cooperate with each other for the same goal./
손을 보다 손을 보다 [soneul boda] /take care of something with one's hands — To fix something that is defective./take care of something with one's hands — To give someone a scolding./
손을 빼다 손을 빼다 [soneul ppaeda] /pull out one's hands — To quit something that one has been working on./
손을 뻗다 손을 뻗다 [soneul ppeodda] /stretch out one's hands — To exert one's power or influence on someone./
손을 쓰다 손을 쓰다 [soneul sseuda] /use one's hands — To take necessary action on something./
손을 씻다[털다] 손을 씻다[털다] [soneul ssisda[teolda]] /wash one's hands; dust one's hands off — To stop committing misdeeds and cut off a relationship with them./
손을 잡다 손을 잡다 [soneul jabda] /hold hands — To work together in concert./
손을 젓다 손을 젓다 [soneul jeosda] /wave one's hands — To deny a certain fact or refuse a proposal./
손이 가다 손이 가다 [soni gada] /One's hands go to something — To keep eating something because it is delicious./One's hands go to something — For something to require a lot of efforts./
손이 닳도록 손이 닳도록 [soni dalhdorog] /to such an extent that one's hands wear out — Very desperately./
손이 많으면 일도 쉽다 손이 많으면 일도 쉽다 [soni manheumyeon ildo swibda] /A great deal of manpower makes it easier to do something — Things can be done easily when shared by many people./
손이 맵다 손이 맵다 [soni maebda] /have a spicy hand — For one's slight hit to hurt very much./have a spicy hand — To work in a shrewd and seamless manner./
손이 여물다 손이 여물다 [soni yeomulda] /have a ripe hand — To work in a very seamless and meticulous manner./
손이 작다 손이 작다 [soni jagda] /have a small hand — To use money or things little by little./
손이 차가운 사람은 심장이 뜨겁다 손이 차가운 사람은 심장이 뜨겁다 [soni chagaun sarameun simjangi tteugeobda] /A person with cold hands has a warm heart — For one to look cold-hearted outside, but be emotional and passionate inside./
손이 크다 손이 크다 [soni keuda] /have a big hand — To use money or things in large quantity./
손익 손익 [sonig] /profit and loss — Gain and loss./
손익 계산서 손익 계산서 [sonig gyesanseo] /statement of profit and loss — A document in which the calculated loss and profit, in comparison with the cost and earning for a certain period, are written down./
손자 손자 [sonja] /grandson — The son of one's son or daughter./
손자국 손자국 [sonjagug] /handprint — Traces of the hand touching or brushing against something./
손잡다 손잡다 [sonjabda] /hold hands — To hold hands./cooperate — To work together by combining efforts./
손잡이 손잡이 [sonjabi] /handle; grip — The part attached to an object that enables a person to hold it easily./
손장난 손장난 [sonjangnan] /fidgeting — The act of moving one's hands or playing with something in one's hands./trick — A simple magic done by using one's hand skill./bet; gambling — The act of playing cards, mah-jong, hwatu, Korean cards, etc., for money or property; a trick for deceiving someone to win such a bet./masturbation — (slang) The act of getting sexual pleasure by stimulating one's own genitals./
손재 손재 [sonjae] /loss of one's wealth — Loss of one's property, or the lost property./
손재간 손재간 [sonjaegan] /dexterity — The ability and technique of making or handling something with one's hands./
손재수 손재수 [sonjaesu] /doomed to lose one's property — Ill luck to lose one's wealth./
손재주 손재주 [sonjaeju] /dexterity — The ability and technique of making or handling something with one's hands./
손전등 손전등 [sonjeondeung] /flashlight — A small lamp that one can carry around./
손주 손주 [sonju] /grandchild — A grandson or granddaughter./
손지갑 손지갑 [sonjigab] /small wallet — A small wallet made to carry around money, cards, etc./
