어깨너머 어깨너머 [eokkaeneomeo] /being over someone's shoulders — A figurative expression for learning something just by watching or listening to others doing something./
어깨동무 어깨동무 [eokkaedongmu] /putting arms around each other's shoulders — A state in which two or more people stand side by side, putting their arms around each other's shoulders./
어깨동무하다 어깨동무하다 [eokkaedongmuhada] /put arms around each other's shoulders — For two or more people to stand side by side and put their arms around each other's shoulders./
어깨를 같이하다 어깨를 같이하다 [eokkaereul gatihada] /share each other's shoulders — To work together for the same purpose./
어깨를 겨누다[겨루다] 어깨를 겨누다[겨루다] [eokkaereul gyeonuda[gyeoruda]] /aim at[compete with] each other's shoulders — To have similar status or power with each other./
어깨를 겯다 어깨를 겯다 [eokkaereul gyeodda] /weave each other's shoulders — To take action in line with each other for the same purpose./
어깨를 나란히 하다 어깨를 나란히 하다 [eokkaereul naranhi hada] /to be shoulder to shoulder — To stand or walk side by side./to be shoulder to shoulder — To have similar status or power with each other./to be shoulder to shoulder — To work together for the same purpose./
어깨를 낮추다 어깨를 낮추다 [eokkaereul najchuda] /lower one's shoulders — To lower oneself in a humble and modest manner./
어깨를 들이밀다[들이대다] 어깨를 들이밀다[들이대다] [eokkaereul deulimilda[deulidaeda]] /stick[thrust] one's shoulders out — To aggressively work on something without sparing oneself./
어깨를 짓누르다 어깨를 짓누르다 [eokkaereul jisnureuda] /press one's shoulders — For duty, responsibility, constraints, etc., to impose a burden on someone./
어깨를 펴다 어깨를 펴다 [eokkaereul pyeoda] /straighten up one's shoulders — To behave in a confident manner./
어깨에 걸머지다 어깨에 걸머지다 [eokkaee geolmeojida] /carry something on one's shoulders — To take heavy responsibility, etc./
어깨에 지다[짊어지다] 어깨에 지다[짊어지다] [eokkaee jida[jilmeojida]] /carry something on one's shoulders — To have the responsibility or duty to do something./
어깨에 힘(을) 주다 어깨에 힘(을) 주다 [eokkaee him(eul) juda] /stiffen one's shoulders — To behave in an arrogant manner./
어깨에 힘이 들어가다 어깨에 힘이 들어가다 [eokkaee himi deuleogada] /have stiff shoulders — To come to behave in an arrogant manner./
어깨춤 어깨춤 [eokkaechum] /moving one's arms up and down — An act of moving one's arms up and down with joy, or dancing with such a movement./
어눌하다 어눌하다 [eonulhada] /awkward in speech; slow in speech — Not fluent in speaking and rather stammering./
어느 어느 [eoneu] /which — Asking for particular something or someone within a number of things./certain — Meaning a thing, person, time, place, etc., that is not certain or does not need to be specified./any — Whatever/certain; some — Certain or a little, or the word that is used to ask about a degree, number or amount./
어느 겨를[틈]에 어느 겨를[틈]에 [eoneu gyeoreul[teum]e] /in which [spare] time — For an unwittingly brief moment./
어느 구석 어느 구석 [eoneu guseog] /which corner — A certain unrecognized place./
어느 누구 어느 누구 [eoneu nugu] /whoever — An expression used to stress everyone./
어느 동네 아이 이름인 줄 아나 어느 동네 아이 이름인 줄 아나 [eoneu dongne ai ireumin jul ana] /Do you think it is the name of some kid from a certain town? — An expression used to sarcastically criticize the attitude of a person who speaks out about something sensitive without reserve./
어느 때고 어느 때고 [eoneu ttaego] /anytime — Regardless of when./anytime — Some time in the future./
어느 세월[천년]에 어느 세월[천년]에 [eoneu sewol[cheonnyeon]e] /in which time[thousand years] — How many years have to pass./
어느 장단에 춤추랴 어느 장단에 춤추랴 [eoneu jangdane chumchurya] /To which tune do I have to dance? — An expression used to describe a situation where there are so many opinions preventing one from making a decision./To which tune do I have to dance? — An expression used to describe a situation where one feels embarrassed at someone's whim./
어느 집 개가 짖느냐 한다 어느 집 개가 짖느냐 한다 [eoneu jib gaega jijneunya handa] /ask 'whose dog is barking?' — An expression used to refer to the attitude of a person who does not listen to what others say./
어느덧 어느덧 [eoneudeos] /already; so soon — Very early while one does not notice./
어느새 어느새 [eoneusae] /already; so soon — Very early while one does not notice./
어두 어두 [eodu] /the beginning of word — The first part of a remark or word./
어두운 밤의 등불 어두운 밤의 등불 [eoduun bamui deungbul] /a lamp in the dark night — Something that is very necessary and important./
어두운색 어두운색 [eoduunsaeg] /dark color — A color that is dark and not vivid./
어두움 어두움 [eoduum] /darkness; dark — A state of being dark or a time when it is dark./
어두컴컴하다 어두컴컴하다 [eodukeomkeomhada] /dim; dimly-lit; dimmish — Dark and dusky./
어둑어둑 어둑어둑 [eodugeodug] /darkly; dimly — In a manner of being so dark that one cannot discern an object clearly./
어둑어둑하다 어둑어둑하다 [eodugeodughada] /dark; dim — Dark to the point that one cannot discern an object clearly./
어둑하다 어둑하다 [eodughada] /dim; fairly dark — Quite dark./knowing little of; gullible — Innocent and naïve, not cunning./
어둠 어둠 [eodum] /darkness; dark — A state of being dark or a time when it is dark./
어둠침침하다 어둠침침하다 [eodumchimchimhada] /gloomy; murky; dim — One's vision being blurred due to darkness./
어둡다 어둡다 [eodubda] /dark; dim — Not bright due to the lack of light./dark; dark-colored — Carrying deepness and heaviness in color./somber; gloomy — Depressed and heavy in mood, facial expression, personality, etc./dark; grim — Hopeless, desperate and helpless./unwise; ignorant — Lacking wisdom and ingenuity as a person or society./challenged; poor — Deficient in eyesight or hearing./dark; gloomy — Emanating a feeling of strangeness, suspicion or trouble./poor; not good; terrible — Not very knowledgeable about a thing or field./blind — Having a desire for something./
어디 어디 [eodi] /where — The word that means a place which one does not know./somewhere — The word that means a place which one does not specify to the listener./anywhere — The word that means a place which is not definite or impossible to specify./something — The word that means something which is hard to pinpoint./great help; great advantage — The word that means an amount, scope, place, etc., is really extraordinary./all — The word that means there is no other possibility at all./all right; fine — An exclamation used to express that one is trying to grab an opportunity or renew his/her resolve to achieve something./on earth — An exclamation used to express that one is wondering about and emphasizing something./all right — An exclamation used to express that one is trying to attract attention from others./
어디 두고 보자 어디 두고 보자 [eodi dugo boja] /Let's wait and see — An expression used to swear that someone will bear undesirable consequences or to warn someone that another person may seek revenge./
어디를 막론하고 어디를 막론하고 [eodireul magronhago] /regardless of where — Everywhere./
어디에다 대고 어디에다 대고 [eodieda daego] /to where; how dare — Disrespectfully to whom on earth./
어때 어때 [eottae] /eottae — An abbreviated word for '어떠해' (a conjugated form of '어떠하다' (be in a state))./
어떠어떠하다 어떠어떠하다 [eotteoeotteohada] /such and such — A thought, feeling, state, situation, etc., being like something in a not-concrete and vague way./
어떠하다 어떠하다 [eotteohada] /such — Being such in one's thoughts, feelings, state, situation, etc./
어떡하다 어떡하다 [eotteoghada] /eotteokada — An abbreviated word for '어떠하게 하다' (deal with)./
어떤 어떤 [eotteon] /what — A word used when asking what characteristic, content, personality, quality, shape, etc., a person or object has./which — A word used when asking what is being chosen among several given people or things./certain; some — A word used to indirectly refer to a subject which does not need to be mentioned./any — A word used to refer to a subject without setting limits to it./
어떻다 어떻다 [eotteohda] /such — Being such in one's thoughts, feelings, state, situation, etc./
어려운 걸음(을) 하다 어려운 걸음(을) 하다 [eoryeoun geoleum(eul) hada] /take a difficult step — To go or come to a place that one cannot easily visit because one is busy or lives far away./take a difficult step — To go or come to a certain place in order to make a decision or request after hesitating for a long time./
어려움 어려움 [eoryeoum] /difficulties; trouble; hardship — A thing that is difficult and painful to do./
어려워하다 어려워하다 [eoryeowohada] /be uncomfortable with — To treat someone carefully because one feels uncomfortable dealing with that person./be difficult; be tough — To regard something as hard or difficult to do./
어련하다 어련하다 [eoryeonhada] /definite; certain; absolute — Making sure that there is no need to worry because things will turn out fine./
어련히 어련히 [eoryeonhi] /definitely; certainly; absolutely — In a definite manner in which there is no need to worry because things will turn out fine./
어렴풋이 어렴풋이 [eoryeompusi] /vaguely; faintly — In the state of one's memory, thought, etc., being dim, not distinct./vaguely; indistinctly — In a state in which something is seen obscurely, not clearly./vaguely; faintly — In a state in which a sound is heard dimly, not distinctly./lightly — In the state of being slightly asleep, not sound asleep./vaguely; dimly — In the state of light being not bright but dim./
어렴풋하다 어렴풋하다 [eoryeompushada] /vague; faint — One's memory, thought, etc., being dim, not distinct./vague; indistinct — Something that is seen being obscure, not clear./vague; faint — A sound that is heard being dim, not distinct./light — Slightly asleep, not sound asleep./vague; dim — Light being not bright but dim./
어렵다 어렵다 [eoryeobda] /difficult; challenging — Very complicated or hard to do./difficult; hard — Being in serious trouble or suffering great hardship./difficult — Not easy to understand some words or a piece of writing./difficult — Hard to make a living because one is poor./difficult — Fastidious and not well-rounded in personality./difficult — Low in possibility./difficult — Awkward and cautious because one feels distant to someone else./
어렵사리 어렵사리 [eoryeobsari] /very difficultly — With great difficulty./
어록 어록 [eorog] /quotations — A collection or written record of remarks of prominent and famous people./
어뢰 어뢰 [eoroe] /torpedo — A fish-shaped bomb used for destroying an enemy's ship by exploding under water./
어루만지다 어루만지다 [eorumanjida] /stroke; smooth down — To touch and pass one's hand over something softly./waft; brush past — For a scent, light, etc., to pass someone or something lightly./soothe — (figurative) To calm down someone with warm words and acts./
어류 어류 [eoryu] /fish — An aquatic, gill-breathing animal that is usually covered with scales and swims with fins, such as mackerel, tuna, shark, etc./
어르고 뺨 치다 어르고 뺨 치다 [eoreugo ppyam chida] /lure someone and slap him/her in the face — To coax someone and do harm to him/her./
어르다 어르다 [eoreuda] /appease — To soothe a young child by gently rocking him/her or entertaining him/her./tease — To play with a person or animal by making fun of him/her./lure — To entice a person into doing something with plausible words./
어르신 어르신 [eoreusin] /your father — (polite form) Another person's father./Sir — (polite form) One's father's friend or an elderly person older than him./
어른 어른 [eoreun] /adult — A fully-grown person./elder — A person who is superior in age, position, etc./grown-up — A married person./elder — A person in the family or group who is respected by others because of his old age and experience./father — (polite form) The father of someone else./
어른 말을 들으면 자다가도 떡이 생긴다 어른 말을 들으면 자다가도 떡이 생긴다 [eoreun maleul deuleumyeon jadagado tteogi saengginda] /You will get tteok, rice cake, if you listen to what elders say — You will gain a variety of benefits if you take your seniors' advice./
어른 뺨치다 어른 뺨치다 [eoreun ppyamchida] /slap an adult in the face — For the understanding, wits and sense of a child to be much better than that of an adult./
어른거리다 어른거리다 [eoreungeorida] /glimmer — For something to keep changing between being recognizable and unrecognizable./shimmer; waver — To move continuously in waves./glimmer — (figurative) To come to one's mind continuously as if one could see something right before one's eyes./
어른대다 어른대다 [eoreundaeda] /glimmer — For something to keep changing between being recognizable and unrecognizable./shimmer; waver — To move continuously in waves./glimmer — (figurative) For someone or something to come to one's mind as if one could see him/her or it right before one's eyes because one misses him/her or it./
어른스럽다 어른스럽다 [eoreunseureobda] /mature — Having a mature quality in one's actions or thoughts despite one's young age./
어른스레 어른스레 [eoreunseure] /maturely — In a mature manner in one's actions or thoughts despite one's young age./
어른어른 어른어른 [eoreuneoreun] /glimmeringly — In the state of continuing to change between being visible and invisible./shimmeringly — In the manner of moving continuously in waves./glimmeringly — (figurative) In the state of something coming to one's mind continuously as if one could see it right before one's eyes./
어른어른하다 어른어른하다 [eoreuneoreunhada] /glimmer — For something to keep changing between being visible and invisible./shimmer; waver — To move continuously in waves./glimmer — (figurative) To come to one's mind continuously as if one could see something right before one's eyes./
어리광 어리광 [eorigwang] /acting like a baby; child's act of coquetry — An act of behaving like a child to please others or be adored by them./
어리굴젓 어리굴젓 [eoriguljeos] /eoriguljeot; spicy salted oysters — A dish made by mixing salted oyster with spices including red pepper powder./
어리다 어리다 [eorida] /gather in; come to — For tears to gather in one's eyes./be filled with; be tinged with — For a certain phenomenon, energy, emotion, etc., to be present or appear slightly./show; be reflected — For light, a shadow, a shape, etc., to appear faintly./emerge — For smoke, fog, clouds, etc., to appear in a mass./young — Low in age./younger — Having a lower age in comparison with someone else./young — Small and weak because the animal or plant has recently been born or budded./childish; immature — Inconsiderate, inexperienced or at a low level./
어리둥절하다 어리둥절하다 [eoridungjeolhada] /confused; confounded; muddled — Dazed from not being familiar with what is happening./
어리바리 어리바리 [eoribari] /absent-mindedly; in a bumbling manner — In a state in which a person's head is not clear or energy is gone and therefore he/she is not able to move his/her body./
어리바리하다 어리바리하다 [eoribarihada] /absent-minded; dazed — Being a state in which a person's head is not clear or energy is gone and therefore he/she is not able to move his/her body./
어리벙벙하다 어리벙벙하다 [eoribeongbeonghada] /confused; confounded; muddled — Very dazed from not being familiar with what is happening./
어리석다 어리석다 [eoriseogda] /silly; stupid; idiotic — One's thought or behavior being not smart or wise./
어린것 어린것 [eoringeos] /little child; one's own child — (endearing) A little child or one's own little child./little thing; little child — (disparaging) A person who is younger than oneself./
어린아이 어린아이 [eorinai] /child; kid — A young boy or girl./
어린애 어린애 [eorinae] /child; kid — An abbreviated word for a young boy or girl./
어린이 어린이 [eorini] /children; kids — A young boy and girl from those aged four or five to elementary school students./
어린이날 어린이날 [eorininal] /Children's day — A day designated in Korea for promotion of children's happiness; it falls on May 5th./
어린이집 어린이집 [eorinijib] /nursery — A place equipped with facilities for taking care of children under the age of six./
어릴 적 버릇은 늙어서까지 간다 어릴 적 버릇은 늙어서까지 간다 [eoril jeog beoreuseun neulgeoseokkaji ganda] /A habit you acquire when you are young is hard to break even when you become old — It is difficult to change a habit that one acquired as a child even if one may try hard during their whole life./
어림 어림 [eorim] /estimation; guess — The act of estimating by guessing, or such estimation or guess./
어림 반 푼어치도 없다 어림 반 푼어치도 없다 [eorim ban puneochido eobsda] /be not even worth half a penny of estimation — To be not worth reconsidering something because it is too nonsensical./
어림없다 어림없다 [eorimeobsda] /impossible; absurd; preposterous — Impossible to be realized./inestimable — Impossible to be guessed because its size, extent, etc., is too large./undue — Beyond one’s capacity./
어림잡다 어림잡다 [eorimjabda] /estimate; guess — To guess the size, amount, etc., of something and roughly figure it out./
어림짐작 어림짐작 [eorimjimjag] /guess; rough guess — An act of guessing and assuming roughly./
어림짐작하다 어림짐작하다 [eorimjimjaghada] /estimate; guess — To make a rough guess./
어림하다 어림하다 [eorimhada] /estimate; guess — To make a rough guess./
어릿광대 어릿광대 [eorisgwangdae] /clown; jester — A person appearing before the start of a circus or piece of theater to create a good atmosphere with funny words and actions./clown — (figurative) A person who makes another laugh with funny words or actions./
어마어마하다 어마어마하다 [eomaeomahada] /enormous; tremendous; huge — Surprisingly large, severe, or marvelous./awesome; amazing — So great as to make someone feel afraid./
어말 어미 어말 어미 [eomal eomi] /final ending — An ending of a word that comes last when a verb, adjective, or predicative particle is conjugated./
어머 어머 [eomeo] /oh my goodness; why — An exclamation used when mainly women are suddenly surprised at or admire an unexpected matter./
어머나 어머나 [eomeona] /oh my goodness; why — An exclamation used when mainly women are suddenly surprised at or admire an unexpected matter with emphasis./
어머니 어머니 [eomeoni] /mother — A word used to refer to or address the woman who has given birth to you./mother — A word used to refer to or address a woman who has a child regarding her child./mother — A word used to refer to or address the woman whom a person treats as though she gave birth to him/her./ma'am — A word used to refer to or address a woman similar in age to another who gave birth to one child in an affectionate manner./mother — (figurative) A person or figure that loves, takes care of, and cares about someone else like her own child./mother — A word used to refer to or address the mother of one's husband in an affectionate manner./mother — (figurative) The origin of something./
어머니 배 속에서 배워 가지고 나오다 어머니 배 속에서 배워 가지고 나오다 [eomeoni bae sogeseo baewo gajigo naoda] /come out of one's mother's womb with the knowledge of something — To be born with the knowledge of something./
어머님 어머님 [eomeonim] /mother; female parent — (honorific) A word used to refer to or address the woman who has procreated as the female parent./someone's mother — (honorific) A word used to refer to or address a woman who has a child regarding her child./mother — (honorific) A word used to refer to or address the woman whom a person treats as though she gave birth to him/her./mother — (honorific) A word used to refer to or address a woman similar in age to the woman who procreated oneself as the female parent in an affectionate manner./mother — (honorific) A word used to refer to or address one's husband's mother in an affectionate manner./mother — (honorific) A word used to refer to or address one's wife's mother in an affectionate manner./
어머머 어머머 [eomeomeo] /oh my goodness; why — An exclamation used when mainly women are suddenly surprised at or admire an unexpected matter./
어멈 어멈 [eomeom] /someone's mother — (slightly respectful) A word used to refer to or address a woman who has a child./daughter — (slightly respectful) A word used by a parent to refer to or address one's married daughter who has a child./daughter-in-law — (slightly respectful) A word used by parents to refer to or address their daughter-in law who has a child./my wife — (slightly respectful) A word used by a man with a child to refer to his wife in the presence of an elderly person./
어명 어명 [eomyeong] /royal command; king's order — An order given from a king./
어묵 어묵 [eomug] /fish cake — Fish paste or fish cake, a dish made by mashing fish flesh, kneading the paste with salt, flour, etc., and cooking it./
어문학 어문학 [eomunhag] /language and literature — A collective term for studies of language and literature./
어물거리다 어물거리다 [eomulgeorida] /move sluggishly — To constantly move slowly little by little./equivocate; hesitate — To say, do, etc., something in a slow, unclear manner./
어물대다 어물대다 [eomuldaeda] /move sluggishly — To constantly move slowly little by little./equivocate; hesitate — To say, do, etc., something in a slow, unclear manner./
어물어물 어물어물 [eomuleomul] /slowly; sluggishly — In a manner of constantly moving slowly little by little./equivocally; hesitantly — In a manner of one's words, actions, etc., being slow and not clear./
어물어물하다 어물어물하다 [eomuleomulhada] /move sluggishly — To constantly move slowly little by little./equivocate; hesitate — To say, do, etc., something in a slow, unclear manner./
어물쩍 어물쩍 [eomuljjeog] /evasively; quibblingly — In a manner of not talking about a subject intentionally without clarifying one's words, actions, etc./
어물쩍거리다 어물쩍거리다 [eomuljjeoggeorida] /evade; quibble — To repeatedly not talk about a subject intentionally without clarifying one's words, actions, etc./
어물쩍대다 어물쩍대다 [eomuljjeogdaeda] /evade; quibble — To repeatedly not talk about a subject intentionally without clarifying one's words, actions, etc./
어물쩍어물쩍 어물쩍어물쩍 [eomuljjeogeomuljjeog] /evasively; quibblingly — In a manner of repeatedly not clarifying one's words, actions, etc., intentionally./
어물쩍어물쩍하다 어물쩍어물쩍하다 [eomuljjeogeomuljjeoghada] /evade; quibble — To repeatedly not talk about a subject intentionally without clarifying one's words, actions, etc./
어물쩍이다 어물쩍이다 [eomuljjeogida] /evade; quibble — Not to clarify one's words, actions, etc., intentionally./
어물쩍하다 어물쩍하다 [eomuljjeoghada] /evade; quibble — To not talk about a subject intentionally without clarifying one's words, actions, etc./
어미 어미 [eomi] /mom; mother — (low form) A word used to refer to or address a woman who has children./daughter — A word used by parents to refer to their daughter who is married and has children./daughter-in-law — A word used by a parent to refer to his/her daughter-in-law when talking to his/her son./wife — (impolite form) A word used by a man who has children to refer to his wife when talking to his elders./mom; mother — (impolite form) A word used by a mother to refer to herself when talking to her children./mother — A female that gave birth to her young./ending of a word — The part that changes in a predicate or predicative particle '이다' (be) when it undergoes an conjugation./
어민 어민 [eomin] /fisherman — A person who catches fish as an occupation./
어버이 어버이 [eobeoi] /parents — Father and mother./
어버이날 어버이날 [eobeoinal] /Parents' Day — A day designated in Korea for expressing gratitude for the love and devotion of parents; it falls on May 8th; children usually give a red carnation to their parents in token of their gratitude./
어법 어법 [eobeob] /grammar; diction — A set of rules in speech./
어벙하다 어벙하다 [eobeonghada] /idiotic; dumb; foolish — A bit stupid, not shrewd./
어부 어부 [eobu] /fisherman — A person who catches fish as an occupation./
어부지리 어부지리 [eobujiri] /fish in troubled waters; playing both ends against the middle — An expression meaning that one gains an advantage effortlessly while the other two people are competing with each other./
어불성설 어불성설 [eobulseongseol] /words without logic; standing to no reason — A remark that does not make sense./
어사 어사 [eosa] /eosa — An undercover royal inspector of the Joseon Dynasty, who used to be dispatched secretly to local areas under the king's command and watch the adminstration of the local governors and life of the people./
어색하다 어색하다 [eosaeghada] /awkward; embarrassing; uneasy — Uncomfortable and not natural because one is facing another person whom one does not know well or has not wanted to see./awkward; unreasonable; absurd — Not having sufficient grounds and illogical./awkward — Not natural because it is against a certain rule, norm, custom, etc./
어서 어서 [eoseo] /quickly; without hesitation; without delay — A word used to hurry someone to work or do something quickly./with pleasure; please — A word used to welcome someone or eagerly ask someone to do something./promptly — Quickly without delay./
어서어서 어서어서 [eoseoeoseo] /without delay; fast; promptly — An adverb used to urge someone to act or do something very quickly./right; welcome; please — An adverb used to welcome someone or offer him/her something very eagerly./
어선 어선 [eoseon] /fishing boat; fishing vessel — A boat or vessel used for catching fish with a net, etc./
어선단 어선단 [eoseondan] /fishing fleet — A group of fishing vessels./
어설프다 어설프다 [eoseolpeuda] /awkward; slovenly; poor — Imperfect and clumsy because one is not familiar with something./poor; incomplete — One's knowledge, behavior, etc., being imperfect./sloppy; careless — Behaving recklessly without thinking carefully or preparing perfectly./light — One's sleep being not deep or one's dream being vague./thin; light — Smoke, smell, etc., being very weak, not thick or strong./
어설피 어설피 [eoseolpi] /awkwardly; in a slovenly manner; poorly — In an imperfect and clumsy  manner because one is not familiar with something./poorly; incompletely — In the state of one's knowledge, behavior, etc., being imperfect./in a sloppy manner; carelessly — In the manner of behaving recklessly without thinking carefully or preparing perfectly./lightly — In the state of one's sleep being not deep or one's dream being vague./thinly; lightly — In the state of smoke, smell, etc., being very weak, not thick or strong./
어수룩하다 어수룩하다 [eosurughada] /naive; unsophisticated — One's way of speaking or behaving being innocent, not shrewd./gullible; half-witted — Very dull and foolish./loose; lax — Not being controlled properly and not strict./
어수선하다 어수선하다 [eosuseonhada] /messy; disordered — Cluttered with jumbled and mixed things./confused; agitated; upset — One's mind or an atmosphere being uneasy, not relaxed./
어순 어순 [eosun] /order of words — The order in which the sentence components such as subject, object and predicate are arranged within a sentence./
어스름 어스름 [eoseureum] /dusk; twilight; gloaming — A state of being slightly dark, or such a time./
어스름하다 어스름하다 [eoseureumhada] /dusky; dim — Slightly dark./
어슬렁거리다 어슬렁거리다 [eoseulreonggeorida] /ramble; wander around; stroll about — For a big sized person or animal to constantly walk around slowly while swaying his/her or its body from side to side./
어슬렁대다 어슬렁대다 [eoseulreongdaeda] /ramble; wander around; stroll about — For a big sized person or animal to constantly walk around slowly while swaying his/her or its body from side to side./
어슬렁어슬렁 어슬렁어슬렁 [eoseulreongeoseulreong] /slowly; sluggishly — In a manner of a big sized person or animal constantly walking around slowly while swaying his/her or its body from side to side./
어슬렁어슬렁하다 어슬렁어슬렁하다 [eoseulreongeoseulreonghada] /ramble; wander around; stroll about — For a big sized person or animal to constantly walk around slowly while swaying his/her or its body from side to side./
어슬렁이다 어슬렁이다 [eoseulreongida] /ramble; wander around; stroll about — For a big sized person or animal to walk around slowly while swaying his/her or its body from side to side./
어슴푸레 어슴푸레 [eoseumpure] /obscurely; faintly — In the state of a light being weak or dim./dimly; obscurely; vaguely — In the state of being indistinct and faint, not being seen or heard clearly./vaguely; faintly — In the state of one's memory or consciousness being dim, not clear./
어슴푸레하다 어슴푸레하다 [eoseumpurehada] /obscure; faint — A light being weak or not bright./dim; obscure; vague — Indistinct and faint, not being seen or heard clearly./vague; faint — One's memory or consciousness being dim, not clear./
어슷비슷하다 어슷비슷하다 [eoseusbiseushada] /being all of a sort — Similar to each other./slanted both ways — Not evenly distributed, leaning to one side or the other./
어슷썰기 어슷썰기 [eoseussseolgi] /diagonal cutting — An act of cutting vegetables such as cucumber, scallion and radish at a slant toward one direction./
어슷하다 어슷하다 [eoseushada] /slanted; tilted — A bit crooked to one side./
어시장 어시장 [eosijang] /fish market — A market that sells marine products including food./
어안 어안 [eoan] /loss — The inside of one's tongue that cannot speak in the face of something totally unexpected./
어안이 막히다 어안이 막히다 [eoani maghida] /have the inside of one's tongue tied — To be dumbfounded by something unexpectedly surprising or weird./
어안이 벙벙하다 어안이 벙벙하다 [eoani beongbeonghada] /have the inside of one's tongue go numb — To be puzzled by something unexpectedly surprising or absurd./
어어 어어 [eoeo] /oh — An exclamation uttered in surprise or in embarrassment when something unexpected happens./yes; yeah — An exclamation uttered when people encourage each other to gather strength for a fight./
어업 어업 [eoeob] /fishery; fishing industry — The industry that catches or breeds fish, clam, laver, seaweed, etc. for making money, or such an occupation./
어여쁘다 어여쁘다 [eoyeoppeuda] /beautiful; lovely — (old-fashioned) Pretty./
어여삐 어여삐 [eoyeoppi] /adorably; cutely; sweetly — In the state of being lovely and pretty to the eye./
어엿이 어엿이 [eoyeosi] /respectably; decently — In a manner of being decent and dignified in one's attitude./
어엿하다 어엿하다 [eoyeoshada] /respectable; decent — Decent and dignified in one's attitude./
어영부영 어영부영 [eoyeongbuyeong] /sloppily; waveringly — In the manner of behaving irresponsibly without a definite or strong will./
어용 어용 [eoyong] /government-patronized; government-controlled; being under the government's thumb — (disparaging) An act of following a person or an institution in power for self-interest./
어우러지다 어우러지다 [eoureojida] /be in harmony; mix well — For several people or things to mingle with each other and become one./mix well — For several things to be in harmony or mix with each other./mingle with — For several people to mix with each other naturally, creating harmony or a certain atmosphere./
어울리다 어울리다 [eoulrida] /harmonize — For several things to be in harmony and unite as one./get along with — To get on well with others or join a certain group and come to be active in the group./be in harmony — (figurative) To have sex with someone./harmonize; be in keeping with; go well with — To be harmonious with each other naturally./
어원 어원 [eowon] /origin of a word; etymology — The original form of a word, or the root that a word is derived from./
어육 어육 [eoyug] /fish; fish meat — The flesh of fish./fish and meat — The flesh of fish and animals./fish and meat — (figurative) The state of having been killed or sacrificed cruelly./
어음 어음 [eoeum] /note; bill — A type of securities where a person promises to pay a certain amount of money in a specific place, or the one that he/she entrusts others with paying./
어이 어이 [eoi] /being unspeakable; being staggering; being absurd — A person or thing that is big beyond one's imagination./how; why — (old-fashioned) How./
어이구 어이구 [eoigu] /oh; whoa; aah — An exclamation uttered when the speaker is aching, exhausted, surprised, or stunned./oh; ooh; ah — An exclamation uttered when the speaker feels very glad or good./oh no; aw — An exclamation uttered when the speaker feels so discouraged or frustrated./
어이구머니 어이구머니 [eoigumeoni] /oh; whoa; aah — An exclamation uttered when the speaker is very severely aching, exhausted, surprised, or stunned./
어이없다 어이없다 [eoieobsda] /absurd; ridiculous; preposterous — Being dumbfounded due to an extremely unexpected occurrence./
어이없이 어이없이 [eoieobsi] /absurdly; ridiculously; preposterously — In a manner of being dumbfounded due to an extremely unexpected occurrence./
어이쿠 어이쿠 [eoiku] /oh; whoa; aah — An exclamation uttered when the speaker is aching, exhausted, vexed, or stunned./oh; wow; yay — An exclamation uttered when the speaker feels very glad or good./oh no; aw — An exclamation uttered when the speaker feels so discouraged or frustrated./
어장 어장 [eojang] /fishing ground; fishery — A place where one goes fishing./fishing ground; fishery — A place where there are abundant marine resources and people can engage in fisheries./
어장이 안되려면 해파리만 끓는다 어장이 안되려면 해파리만 끓는다 [eojangi andoeryeomyeon haepariman kkeulhneunda] /A failing fishing ground tends to be infested with jellyfish — You may suffer a lot of difficulties and troubles if things are destined to go wrong./
어저께 어저께 [eojeokke] /yesterday — The day preceding today./yesterday — On the day before today./
어절 어절 [eojeol] /word segment — Each and every single word segment that makes up a sentence./
어정쩡 어정쩡 [eojeongjjeong] /vaguely; equivocally — In an obscure manner in speaking or behaving./awkwardly; in a perplexed way — In the state of one's behavior being unnatural; in the state of feeling embarrassed because one cannot decide what to do./with doubts — With an attitude of being anxious about something because one cannot trust it./
어정쩡하다 어정쩡하다 [eojeongjjeonghada] /indefinite; ambiguous; obscure — One's words or behavior being not definite./awkward; uncomfortable — Unnatural or embarrassed because one can do neither this nor that./doubtful; suspicious — Someone or something making one feel uneasy because he/she or it is dubious./
어제 어제 [eoje] /yesterday — The day before the present day./yesterday — The time that has gone by./yesterday — On the day before today./
어제 오늘 할 것 없이 어제 오늘 할 것 없이 [eoje oneul hal geos eobsi] /regardless of yesterday and today — Regardless of dates./
어제(가) 다르고 오늘(이) 다르다 어제(가) 다르고 오늘(이) 다르다 [eoje(ga) dareugo oneul(i) dareuda] /Things were different yesterday and things are different today — For the speed of change to be very fast./
어제가 옛날(이다) 어제가 옛날(이다) [eojega yesnal(ida)] /Yesterday is the old days — For the change of something to be very big because it changes very fast./
어제와 오늘이 다르다 어제와 오늘이 다르다 [eojewa oneuli dareuda] /Yesterday and today are different — For something today to be not like what it was yesterday because there has been a change overnight./
어제저녁 어제저녁 [eojejeonyeog] /last evening — The previous evening./
어젯밤 어젯밤 [eojesbam] /last night — The previous night./
어조 어조 [eojo] /tone — Emotions or thoughts expressed in words./intonation — The rise and fall of a voice./
어족 어족 [eojog] /fish — An aquatic, gill-breathing animal that is usually covered with scales and swims with fins, such as mackerel, tuna, shark, etc./language family — A group of languages derived from the same root./
어종 어종 [eojong] /fish species — The species of fish./
어줍다 어줍다 [eojubda] /awkward; clumsy; unskilled — One's way of speaking or behaving being clumsy and awkward because one is not accustomed to something./stiff; dull — One's movements being dull because a part of one's body is not free./awkward — Feeling nervous because one does not know what to do, or feeling awkward because one feels sorry for something./
어중간하다 어중간하다 [eojungganhada] /midway; halfway — Being in the middle between two points./ambiguous; vague; noncommittal — Indecisive./awkward; not completely fitting — The time or period being not suitable to do either this or that./incomplete — Not meeting a standard perfectly but quite close to it./
어중이떠중이 어중이떠중이 [eojungitteojungi] /all sorts of people; Tom, Dick and Harry; the rabble — (disparaging) A crowd of average, ordinary people from everywhere./
어지간하다 어지간하다 [eojiganhada] /fair; considerable — Being of the average level or exceeding the average level./tolerable; passable — A degree or situation being not totally against a standard./tractable; generous; mild — Meek, not fastidious, unlike what one has thought./finicky; picky — Someone being hard to deal with because he/she is not docile./
어지간히 어지간히 [eojiganhi] /fairly; considerably — In the state of being equal to or slightly above the average level./tolerably; passably — In the state of a degree or situation being not totally against a standard./fastidiously — In the state of someone being hard to deal with because he/she is not docile./considerably; very much; greatly — Far more than the average level./
어지러이 어지러이 [eojireoi] /dizzily; giddily; dazedly — In the state of being so dazed as to unable to balance oneself./confusingly — In the state of being too jumbled and intermingled to be sorted out./messily — In the state of being messy because things are scattered all over a place./in a disorderly state — In the state of a society being in chaos without order./
어지럼증 어지럼증 [eojireomjeung] /dizziness; vertigo — A symptom characterized by a lightheaded feeling and blurred vision./
어지럽다 어지럽다 [eojireobda] /dizzy; giddy — Unable to balance oneself because it feels as if the world is revolving around oneself./confused; distracted — Too jumbled and intermingled to be sorted out./disorderly; disordered — A society being in chaos without order./indecent; improper; immoral — One's way of speaking or behaving being not right or decent./cluttered; untidy — Messy because things are scattered all over a place./
어지럽히다 어지럽히다 [eojireobhida] /make someone dizzy; make someone giddy — To make someone unable to balance himself/herself because it feels as if the world is revolving around himself/herself./confuse; disturb — To make it hard to catch the point by mingling or mixing all things./disturb; disorder — To make a society be in chaos without order./mess up; clutter; disarray — To make a place messy by scattering things all over the place./
어지르다 어지르다 [eojireuda] /mess up; litter — To intertwine or jumble up an organized matter or objects./
어질다 어질다 [eojilda] /benign; kind-hearted; benevolent — Generous, humane, wise, and virtuous./
어질어질 어질어질 [eojileojil] /giddily; dizzily — In a state in which one feels dizzy and wobbly./
어질어질하다 어질어질하다 [eojileojilhada] /giddy; dizzy — Feeling dizzy and wobbly in one's mind./
